{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-80079aecb3a138dc", "type": "tool_result", "content": "<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (65.5KB). Full output saved to: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa/tool-results/b3fazv1uw.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nLooking in indexes: http://nexus.sii.shaipower.online/repository/pypi/simple\nRequirement already satisfied: pymupdf in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (1.27.2.3)\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nSemBridge: Semantic Token Anchoring for Continuous-Latent\nAutoregressive Speech Generation\nHanke Xie1,2∗†, Haopeng Lin2†, Jiale Qian2, Dake Guo1, Yuepeng Jiang1, Zhichao Wang2, Wenxiao\nCao2, Jingbin Hu1, Guobin Ma1, Wenhao Li1, Huakang Chen1, Chengyou Wang1, Ming Tao2,\nZhonghua Fu1, Lei Xie1‡, Xinsheng Wang2‡\n1Audio, Speech and Language Processing Group (ASLP@NPU), School of Software\nNorthwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, China\n2Soul AI Lab, China\nhkxie@mail.nwpu.edu.cn, lxie@nwpu.edu.cn, wangxinsheng@soulapp.cn\nAbstract\nContinuous-latent\nautoregressive\nspeech\ngeneration\nhas\nemerged as a promising alternative to discrete-token modeling\nby avoiding quantization loss and preserving richer acoustic\ninformation. However, continuous acoustic targets do not ex-\npose linguistic structure as explicit token-level prediction tar-\ngets. Consequently, the autoregressive language model (LM)\nmust acquire linguistic structure indirectly through acous-\ntic prediction, which can compromise the content fidelity of\ngenerated speech. We propose SemBridge, a training-only\nsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\nautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge uses discrete se-\nmantic tokens to directly supervise autoregressive LM states\nand employs a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE to organize\nthe continuous target space under the same semantic refer-\nence. The semantic supervision is used only during train-\ning, while inference remains entirely continuous. We evalu-\nate SemBridge on zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) and score-\nconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS). Across multi-\nple benchmarks, SemBridge improves content accuracy, as\nmeasured by word and character error rates (WER/CER),\nwhile maintaining competitive speaker similarity and percep-\n...\n</persisted-output>", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa", "uuid": "6214db2d-31e3-4f16-9e90-fcf331a6a75f", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T07:00:28.629Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "Looking in indexes: http://nexus.sii.shaipower.online/repository/pypi/simple\nRequirement already satisfied: pymupdf in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (1.27.2.3)\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nSemBridge: Semantic Token Anchoring for Continuous-Latent\nAutoregressive Speech Generation\nHanke Xie1,2∗†, Haopeng Lin2†, Jiale Qian2, Dake Guo1, Yuepeng Jiang1, Zhichao Wang2, Wenxiao\nCao2, Jingbin Hu1, Guobin Ma1, Wenhao Li1, Huakang Chen1, Chengyou Wang1, Ming Tao2,\nZhonghua Fu1, Lei Xie1‡, Xinsheng Wang2‡\n1Audio, Speech and Language Processing Group (ASLP@NPU), School of Software\nNorthwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, China\n2Soul AI Lab, China\nhkxie@mail.nwpu.edu.cn, lxie@nwpu.edu.cn, wangxinsheng@soulapp.cn\nAbstract\nContinuous-latent\nautoregressive\nspeech\ngeneration\nhas\nemerged as a promising alternative to discrete-token modeling\nby avoiding quantization loss and preserving richer acoustic\ninformation. However, continuous acoustic targets do not ex-\npose linguistic structure as explicit token-level prediction tar-\ngets. Consequently, the autoregressive language model (LM)\nmust acquire linguistic structure indirectly through acous-\ntic prediction, which can compromise the content fidelity of\ngenerated speech. We propose SemBridge, a training-only\nsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\nautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge uses discrete se-\nmantic tokens to directly supervise autoregressive LM states\nand employs a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE to organize\nthe continuous target space under the same semantic refer-\nence. The semantic supervision is used only during train-\ning, while inference remains entirely continuous. We evalu-\nate SemBridge on zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) and score-\nconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS). Across multi-\nple benchmarks, SemBridge improves content accuracy, as\nmeasured by word and character error rates (WER/CER),\nwhile maintaining competitive speaker similarity and percep-\ntual quality. Experimental results demonstrate that explicit\nsemantic-token supervision for autoregressive state learning\nis an effective and general direction for continuous speech\ngeneration. Speech samples are available.1 The model code\nand checkpoints will be available at https://github.com/ASLP-\nlab/SemBridge.\nIntroduction\nAutoregressive (AR) models have emerged as a dominant\nparadigm for speech synthesis. (Wang et al. 2023; Anas-\ntassiou et al. 2024). Most AR speech generation systems\nadopt a language-modeling formulation, representing speech\nas discrete token sequences and formulating generation as\nstandard next-token prediction. Recent AR systems fur-\nther advance discrete speech representations by introducing\n1https://tiamojames.github.io/SemBridge_Demo/\n∗Work done during an internship at Soul AI Lab.\n†These authors contributed equally.\n‡Corresponding author.\nlow-rate and semantically structured speech tokens through\npretrained speech representations or hierarchical semantic–\nacoustic modeling (Du et al. 2024b, 2025; Hu et al. 2026;\nZhang et al. 2024a). These designs provide compact and lin-\nguistically meaningful prediction targets, reducing the text–\nspeech semantic gap and improving autoregressive model-\ning. However, discrete representations inevitably introduce\nquantization constraints, limiting their capacity to preserve\nfine-grained acoustic details required for high-fidelity speech\ngeneration. Continuous representations avoid quantization\nloss while providing a richer and more expressive acoustic\ntarget space. Consequently, continuous-latent autoregressive\ngeneration has emerged as a promising direction for high-\nfidelity speech synthesis (Meng et al. 2025; Jia et al. 2025;\nXia et al. 2026).\nContinuous-latent autoregressive models generate speech\nby predicting continuous acoustic representations, such as\nmel-spectrograms or learned acoustic latents, often using\ndiffusion- or flow-based next-patch generation to model com-\nplex acoustic distributions\n(Jia et al. 2025; Peng et al.\n2026; Zhou et al. 2025b). These continuous targets preserve\nrich acoustic information while implicitly encoding linguis-\ntic content. Unlike discrete semantic tokens, however, they\ndo not provide explicit semantic targets at each generation\nstep. Consequently, the autoregressive LM is required to infer\nlinguistic structure indirectly from the continuous generation\nobjective, without explicit token-level semantic supervision.\nRecent studies have explored various strategies for in-\ncorporating semantic information into continuous speech\ngeneration. Existing approaches mainly leverage continuous\nsemantic representations extracted from pretrained speech\nmodels to enhance either target representations or autore-\ngressive hidden states (Wang et al. 2026; An et al. 2026).\nWhile these methods demonstrate the importance of seman-\ntic modeling for continuous generation, they formulate se-\nmantic supervision as continuous feature alignment. In con-\ntrast, discrete semantic tokens organize linguistic content into\ndiscrete semantic units and provide explicit prediction tar-\ngets for autoregressive language modeling. Such discretiza-\ntion makes clusterable linguistic structure explicit while sup-\npressing linguistically irrelevant variation retained in con-\narXiv:2608.07462v1  [eess.AS]  7 Aug 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\ntinuous speech features (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen, Sagot,\nand Dupoux 2022). However, leveraging discrete semantic\ntokens to explicitly supervise continuous-latent autoregres-\nsive states remains underexplored. Other approaches intro-\nduce hierarchical or semi-discrete architectures to combine\nsemantic and acoustic modeling (Zhou et al. 2025b; Yu et al.\n2025); however, they modify the original continuous genera-\ntion interface by introducing additional semantic generation\ncomponents.\nTo address this gap, we introduce SemBridge, a training-\nonly semantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-\nlatent autoregressive speech generation. At its core, Sem-\nBridge uses discrete semantic-token labels to provide direct\nsemantic supervision for autoregressive LM states, while\ncontinuous latent patches remain the sole generated and\nrecurrent variables. A complementary Semantic-Aligned\nAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE) further aligns continuous acoustic\nlatents with token-level embeddings from the same semantic\ntokenizer, establishing a shared semantic reference for latent\nrepresentation learning and state supervision.\nWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot text-to-\nspeech (TTS) and further assess its transferability to score-\nconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS) (Liu et al. 2022;\nZhang et al. 2022; Qian et al. 2026). Across multiple\nTTS and SVS benchmarks, SemBridge consistently reduces\nWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker similar-\nity and perceptual quality. Controlled ablations show that\nsemantic-token anchoring provides complementary gains be-\nyond target-space alignment alone, while layer-wise analyses\nreveal a trade-off between content accuracy and synthesis\nquality.\nOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n• We propose SemBridge, a training-only semantic-token\nanchoring framework that directly supervises autoregres-\nsive LM states with discrete semantic-token labels while\npreserving continuous-only generation during inference.\n• We introduce a unified semantic supervision scheme\nbased on a shared frozen semantic tokenizer: token-level\nembeddings align continuous acoustic latents through\nSA-VAE, while discrete semantic-token labels explicitly\nsupervise autoregressive LM hidden states.\n• Experiments on zero-shot TTS and score-conditioned\nSVS\nshow\nthat\nSemBridge\nconsistently\nimproves\nWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker sim-\nilarity and perceptual quality. Ablations further confirm\nthe complementary benefits of latent alignment and state\nanchoring and reveal a trade-off effect of anchoring depth.\nRelated Work\nDiscrete-Token\nAutoregressive\nSpeech\nGeneration.\nModern LM-based speech systems commonly represent\nspeech as discrete token sequences produced by semantic\nor neural-codec tokenizers. AudioLM, VALL-E, and Seed-\nTTS formulate speech generation as autoregressive sequence\nmodeling over discrete speech representations, enabling scal-\nable generation and zero-shot adaptation (Borsos et al. 2022;\nWang et al. 2023; Anastassiou et al. 2024). Recent work\nincreasingly explores low-rate and semantically informed to-\nkens to shorten speech sequences and strengthen linguistic\nmodeling. SpeechTokenizer distributes semantic and acous-\ntic information across multiple quantization levels, while\nWavTokenizer explores a compact single-codebook repre-\nsentation (Zhang et al. 2024a; Ji et al. 2025). Self-supervised\nspeech studies further show that learned representations ex-\nhibit clusterable phonetic structure, while discrete-unit analy-\nses demonstrate that discretization can suppress linguistically\nirrelevant variation and provide effective prediction targets\nfor spoken language modeling (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen,\nSagot, and Dupoux 2022). Nevertheless, quantization intro-\nduces an information bottleneck that can limit the preserva-\ntion of fine-grained acoustic details.\nContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Speech Generation.\nContinuous-latent approaches avoid discrete quantization by\ndirectly modeling mel-spectrograms, learned acoustic la-\ntents, or latent distributions. MELLE autoregressively gen-\nerates continuous mel-spectrogram frames, while KALL-E\nmodels the distribution of future continuous latents (Meng\net al. 2025; Xia et al. 2026). Recent methods further combine\ncausal sequence modeling with diffusion- or flow-based local\ngeneration. DiTAR separates inter-patch dependency model-\ning from intra-patch acoustic generation through a causal\nLM and a local diffusion transformer (Jia et al. 2025), while\nVibeVoiceuseslow-rate continuousrepresentationsandnext-\npatch diffusion for long-form multi-speaker generation (Peng\net al. 2026). These approaches preserve an expressive contin-\nuous acoustic target space, but their autoregressive states are\ntrained primarily through continuous generation objectives.\nSemantic Guidance for Continuous Autoregressive Gen-\neration.\nRecent methods introduce semantic guidance into\ncontinuous generation through pretrained speech representa-\ntions. SemaVoice aligns continuous VAE latents with high-\nlevel features from a frozen speech foundation model, im-\nproving semantic structure in the acoustic target space be-\nfore autoregressive training (Wang et al. 2026). MELA-TTS\ninstead regularizes autoregressive states by aligning them\nwith continuous ASR features (An et al. 2026). These meth-\nods transfer semantic information through continuous feature\nalignment at the target and predictor levels, respectively. Vox-\nCPM adopts a semi-discrete semantic-prosodic bottleneck\ntogether with a residual pathway, retaining an explicit seman-\ntic modeling branch during inference (Zhou et al. 2025b).\nMethod\nOverview\nSemBridge is built on a continuous-latent autoregressive\nbackbone that generates acoustic latent patches conditioned\non symbolic inputs and preceding acoustic history. Its core\nmechanism is semantic-token anchoring, which uses dis-\ncrete semantic-token labels to provide direct supervision\nfor selected autoregressive LM states. A complementary\nSemantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) organizes the\ncontinuous acoustic target space under the same semantic\nreference. Together, the two objectives introduce semantic\nstructure into both the predictive LM states and the contin-\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nSemantic   Anchoring\nSemantic\nEncoder\nAcoustic\nEncoder\nVariational\nPosterior\nVQ\nSemantic Token\nAlign Loss\nAcoustic \nReconstruction\nLoss\nAcoustic\nDecoder\nSemantic\nProjector\nDecoder-Only Transformer\nUnified Prefix\nTask\nText\nMiDi\nPatch Enc\nPatch Enc\n(A) Semantic-Aligned Acoustic Latent Tokenizer\n(B) SemBridge: Next Acoustic-Latent Prediction \nLocDiT\nLocDiT\nLocDiT\nSemantic Feature\nAcoustic Feature\nSemantic Token\nAcoustic Latent\nPatch Enc\nPatch Latent \nText Sequence\nText Embedding\nTraining only\nTraining & Inference\nCe Loss \n \nSemantic Head\nHidden State \nInference only\nFigure 1: Overview of the two-stage SemBridge framework. (A) Stage I trains SA-VAE to reconstruct waveforms while aligning\ncontinuous acoustic patches with embeddings from a frozen semantic tokenizer; their token IDs are retained as Stage-II targets.\n(B) Stage II trains a continuous-latent autoregressive generator: a causal LM encodes symbolic conditions and preceding acoustic\npatches, LocDiT predicts the next patch, and a semantic head supervises a LM hidden layer under the same causal shift.\nuous targets they model, while retaining continuous latent\npatches as the sole generated and recurrent variables.\nWe use an open-source pretrained GLM-4 tokenizer(Zeng\net al. 2024) operating at 12.5 Hz. Given a training waveform\nx, the tokenizer produces a sequence of token-level semantic\nembeddings and their corresponding discrete token IDs:\n(E, S) = SGLM(x), E = {et}T\nt=1, S = {st}T\nt=1,\n(1)\nwhere et denotes the continuous embedding associated with\nthe semantic token st, and st ∈{1, . . . , V } with vocabu-\nlary size V = 16,384. The embeddings and token IDs pro-\nvide two complementary forms of supervision: et is used for\ncontinuous target-space alignment, whereas st serves as the\ncategorical target for LM-state anchoring.\nThe SA-VAE encoder produces frame-level continuous\nacoustic latents A = {an}2T\nn=1 at 25 Hz. We group every two\nconsecutive frames into one autoregressive acoustic patch:\nzt = Concat (a2t−1, a2t) ,\nt = 1, . . . , T.\n(2)\nThe resulting acoustic patch sequence Z = {zt}T\nt=1 therefore\nhas the same 12.5-Hz rate as the semantic tokenizer outputs.\nThis establishes a one-to-one temporal correspondence:\nzt ←→(et, st) .\n(3)\nAccordingly, each continuous patch is aligned with a token-\nlevel embedding during SA-VAE training, and the LM state\nresponsible for predicting that patch is supervised by the\ncorresponding discrete token ID during generator training.\nTraining proceeds in two stages as shown in Figure 1.\nStage I trains SA-VAE with acoustic reconstruction and se-\nmantic target-alignment objectives. Stage II freezes SA-VAE\nand the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer, and trains the continuous\nautoregressive generator with its original generation objec-\ntives together with semantic-token anchoring. Although SA-\nVAE is trained first, the defining component of SemBridge\nis the Stage-II token-level supervision applied directly to au-\ntoregressive LM states.\nContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Backbone\nGiven the continuous acoustic patch sequence Z\n=\n{z1, . . . , zT } produced by the frozen SA-VAE, SemBridge\nautoregressively models speech generation conditioned on a\nsymbolic input c and an optional acoustic prompt r:\np(Z | c, r) =\nT\nY\nt=1\np (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n(4)\nAutoregressive\nContext\nModeling.\nFollowing\nrecent\ncontinuous-latent autoregressive frameworks\n(Jia et al.\n2025), the generator consists of PatchEnc, a causal LM, and\na local diffusion transformer, denoted as LocDiT. PatchEnc\nmaps the acoustic prompt and preceding continuous patches\ninto acoustic context representations, which are combined\nwith the symbolic conditions and processed by the causal\nLM. At the position responsible for predicting zt, the hidden\nstate produced by Transformer layer ℓis:\nh(ℓ)\nt\n= H(ℓ)\nθ\n(c, r, z<t) ,\nℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n(5)\nThe causal construction ensures that h(ℓ)\nt\nonly depends on the\nsymbolic conditions, prompt context, and preceding acoustic\npatches. The final-layer state provides the global condition\nfor continuous next-patch generation, while a selected hid-\nden layer is additionally supervised by the semantic-token\nanchoring objective described in Section Semantic-Token\nAnchoring.\nConditional Continuous Patch Generation.\nThe normal-\nized final LM state\ngt = RMSNorm\n\u0010\nh(L)\nt\n\u0011\n(6)\nconditions LocDiT together with the preceding acoustic\npatch zt−1. Rather than directly regressing zt, LocDiT mod-\nels its conditional distribution using flow matching:\nLFM = E\nh\n∥vΘ (ezt,τ, τ; gt, zt−1) −v⋆\nt ∥2\n2\ni\n,\n(7)\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nwhere τ denotes the flow time, ezt,τ is the corresponding\nintermediate flow state, and v⋆\nt is the target velocity field.\nA lightweight stop predictor attached to the final LM state\ndetermines sequence termination.\nSemantic-Token Anchoring\nCausally Aligned Semantic Prediction.\nLet h(ℓ)\nt\ndenote\nthe post-block hidden state produced by Transformer layer ℓ\nat the autoregressive position responsible for predicting the\nnext continuous patch zt:\nh(ℓ)\nt\n= H(ℓ)\nθ\n(c, r, z<t) ,\nℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n(8)\nBecause the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer and the autoregres-\nsive acoustic patches share the same 12.5-Hz temporal rate,\nthe semantic token st is associated with the same target patch\nzt. Under the causal shift used for next-patch prediction, the\nstate h(ℓ)\nt\nhas access only to the symbolic conditions, prompt\ncontext, and preceding acoustic patches z<t. It does not ob-\nserve either the target patch zt or its semantic label st.\nThe same autoregressive state is therefore trained to sup-\nport two complementary predictions:\nh(L)\nt\n−→zt,\nh(ℓ⋆)\nt\n−→st,\n(9)\nwhere the final-layer state conditions continuous next-patch\ngeneration, while a selected intermediate or final layer ℓ⋆\nis anchored by the corresponding semantic-token label. Se-\nmantic tokens are used only as supervision targets and never\nenter the autoregressive context.\nDepth-Selective\nToken\nClassification.\nWe\nattach\na\nlightweight semantic prediction head fϕ to the post-block\nhidden state at a selected Transformer depth ℓ⋆. The condi-\ntional semantic-token distribution is:\nqϕ\n\u0010\nst | h(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\n= softmax\n\u0010\nfϕ\n\u0010\nh(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\u0011\n.\n(10)\nUnlike continuous feature alignment, this objective does not\nrequire the autoregressive state to reproduce the full geome-\ntry of a teacher representation. Instead, it assigns an explicit\nsemantic classification target to each state responsible for\ncontinuous next-patch prediction.\nThe anchoring objective is computed over all valid acous-\ntic prediction positions:\nLsem = −1\n|T |\nX\nt∈T\nlog qϕ\n\u0010\nst | h(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\n,\n(11)\nwhere T denotes the set of valid autoregressive acoustic\npositions after excluding padded positions. We allow the at-\ntachment depth ℓ⋆to vary and study its effect experimentally.\nJoint\nContinuous\nand\nSemantic\nObjectives.\nThe\nsemantic-token objective is jointly optimized with the origi-\nnal continuous-latent generation losses:\nLgen = LFM + λsemLsem + λstopLstop.\n(12)\nThe flow-matching objective trains continuous next-patch\ngeneration, while Lsem updates the semantic head and the\nautoregressive LM states. The SA-VAE and GLM-4 semantic\ntokenizer remain frozen throughout Stage II. Consequently,\nthe semantic objective acts as a state-level training constraint\nwithout changing the acoustic target or introducing discrete\nvariables into the recurrent generation history.\nContinuous-Only\nInference.\nAt\ninference,\nneither\nsemantic-token labels nor the semantic prediction head\nare required. Generation follows the original continuous\nPatchEnc–LM–LocDiT pathway:\np(Z | c, r) =\nT\nY\nt=1\np (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n(13)\nContinuous latent patches remain the only generated vari-\nables and recurrent acoustic history. The semantic-token\nbranch therefore changes how the autoregressive states are\nlearned, rather than how speech is generated.\nTarget-Space Alignment with SA-VAE\nComplementary to state-level semantic-token anchoring, we\nintroduce a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) to\nimprove the semantic correspondence of the continuous\nacoustic target space. SA-VAE remains a continuous Gaus-\nsian VAE and uses a frozen semantic tokenizer as its se-\nmantic reference. Given an input waveform x, the tokenizer\nextracts token-level semantic embeddings e = Sfeat(x). A\nlightweight projection maps the continuous acoustic latent\nrepresentations into the semantic feature space, and seman-\ntic correspondence is optimized through:\nLalign = Dsem (Pz(z), sg [e]) ,\n(14)\nwhere Pzdenote lightweight alignment mappings, sg(·) stops\ngradients through the frozen semantic tokenizer, and Dsem\ncombines cosine alignment with a weak L1 constraint.\nThe complete Stage-I objective is:\nLSA-VAE = LVAE + λalignLalign.\n(15)\nThis objective encourages patch-level semantic correspon-\ndence without imposing a discrete bottleneck on the acoustic\nrepresentation. After Stage I training, SA-VAE is frozen and\nprovides continuous acoustic targets for generator training.\nTarget-space alignment and state-level anchoring use dif-\nferent forms of supervision from the same semantic tok-\nenizer. SA-VAE aligns zt with the continuous embedding\net, whereas semantic-token anchoring uses the correspond-\ning discrete ID st to supervise the LM state responsible for\npredicting zt. Their shared semantic identity and temporal\ncorrespondence make the two objectives complementary.\nTask Conditioning for TTS and SVS\nSemantic-token anchoring is applied to the autoregressive\nLM states responsible for continuous next-patch prediction\nand is shared across TTS and SVS. TTS uses text and lan-\nguage conditions, whereas score-conditioned SVS addition-\nally incorporates lyrics and score-derived pitch and duration;\nboth tasks otherwise share the same continuous acoustic rep-\nresentation, PatchEnc–LM–LocDiT backbone, and anchor-\ning objective. SVS therefore provides a more demanding\nextension for examining whether semantic-token supervi-\nsion improves lyric intelligibility under explicit musical con-\nstraints without sacrificing the fine-grained acoustic variation\nand expressiveness of singing.\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nModel\nParams Data (h)\nContent Consistency ↓| Speaker Similarity ↑\nSeed-TTS-Eval\nCV3-EVAL\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nEN-Hard\nReported and open-source systems\nF5-TTS\n0.3B\n100k\n1.53 | 0.760\n2.00 | 0.670\n8.67 | 0.713\n5.47 | –\n8.90 | –\n– | –\n– | –\nCosyVoice2\n0.5B\n170k\n1.38 | 0.757\n3.09 | 0.659\n6.83 | 0.724\n4.08 | –\n6.32 | –\n12.58 | 0.726\n11.96 | 0.667\nCosyVoice3-1.5B\n1.5B\n1.0M\n1.12 | 0.781\n2.22 | 0.720\n5.83 | 0.758\n3.91 | –\n4.99 | –\n9.77 | 0.785\n10.55 | 0.761\nIndexTTS2\n1.5B\n–\n1.03 | 0.765\n2.23 | 0.706\n7.12 | 0.755\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nHiggsAudio-v2\n3B\n10M\n1.50 | 0.740\n2.44 | 0.677\n55.07 | 0.656\n3.89 | –\n5.24 | –\n14.14 | 0.786\n9.04 | 0.759\nContinuous-latent autoregressive systems\nDiTAR\n0.6B\n–\n1.02 | 0.753\n1.69 | 0.735\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nMELA-TTS\n0.8B\n170k\n0.95 | 0.720\n2.45 | 0.590\n7.75 | 0.710\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nVoxCPM-Emilia\n0.6B\n100k\n1.11 | 0.740\n2.34 | 0.681\n12.46 | 0.698\n4.47 | –\n5.23 | –\n22.20 | 0.626\n10.00 | 0.626\nVoxCPM\n0.6B\n1.8M\n0.93 | 0.772\n1.85 | 0.729\n8.87 | 0.730\n3.40 | –\n4.04 | –\n12.90 | 0.661\n7.89 | 0.643\nSemaVoice\n1.5B\n100k\n1.32 | 0.728\n1.91 | 0.657\n9.37 | 0.687\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nVibeVoice∗\n1.5B\n–\n2.78 | 0.686\n5.28 | 0.588\n29.43 | 0.583\n5.16 | 0.702\n6.96 | 0.560\n15.94 | 0.637\n7.89 | 0.586\nVoxCPM2\n2B\n2M\n0.97 | 0.795\n1.84 | 0.753\n8.13 | 0.753\n3.65 | –\n5.00 | –\n8.55 | –\n8.48 | –\nSemBridge\n0.8B\n120k\n0.95 | 0.758\n1.81 | 0.699\n9.79 | 0.717\n3.34 | 0.757\n4.22 | 0.658\n10.58 | 0.717\n6.35 | 0.619\nTable 1: Objective evaluation on Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL. Each result is reported as content consistency error | speaker\nsimilarity, where Chinese subsets use CER, English subsets use WER, and speaker similarity is measured by SIM. Lower\ncontent error and higher SIM indicate better performance. “–” denotes unavailable results. Bold and underlined values indicate\nthe best and second-best results, respectively. Tied second-best results are both underlined. ∗denotes results obtained through\nour evaluation.\nExperiments\nExperimental Setup\nDatasets.\nWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot\ntext-to-speech (TTS), with score-conditioned singing voice\nsynthesis (SVS) as a cross-task extension. SA-VAE is trained\nseparately on 25K hours of audio, including 20K hours of\nspeech sampled from public corpora and a 5K-hour subset\nof our internal singing data. All matched TTS models and\ncomponent ablations are trained on the 100K-hour bilingual\nopen-source VoxBox corpus (Wang et al. 2025). For joint\nTTS and SVS training, we augment VoxBox with the full\n20K hours of internal singing data, resulting in a 120K-hour\nmultitask corpus. We use the 100K-hour models for con-\ntrolled component attribution and the 120K-hour model for\nsystem-level comparison and SVS evaluation. Further details\non corpus composition and preprocessing are provided in the\nsupplementary material.\nModel Architecture.\nSemBridge consists of a continuous-\nlatent autoregressive backbone, a frozen Semantic-Aligned\nAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE), and training-time semantic super-\nvision modules. The causal LM contains 32 Transformer\nblocks with a hidden size of 1,024 and 16 attention heads.\nPatchEnc and LocDiT each contain 8 blocks with the same\nhidden size and number of heads. SA-VAE is a causal con-\nvolutional Gaussian VAE that encodes 44.1 kHz waveforms\ninto 64-dimensional continuous latent frames at 25 Hz with-\nout vector quantization. The frozen semantic tokenizer op-\nerates at 12.5 Hz with a vocabulary of 16,384 tokens. Dur-\ning Stage I, a lightweight projection aligns SA-VAE acous-\ntic patches with token-level semantic embeddings. During\nStage II, the corresponding discrete token labels supervise\nautoregressive LM states. We denote a semantic head at-\ntached after Transformer block k as Anchor@k.\nExcluding the frozen SA-VAE, the continuous generation\nbackbone contains 800.15M trainable parameters. SA-VAE\ncontains 86.48M parameters, while the alignment projec-\ntion and semantic anchoring module add 2.43M and 16.79M\nparameters, respectively. The alignment projection and se-\nmantic head are used only during training and are removed\nat inference.\nTraining Details.\nWe train SA-VAE independently for\n300K updates on 3.0-second audio segments with a batch\nsize of 48 using 8 NVIDIA H20 GPUs. We then train Sem-\nBridge for 300K updates using 16 NVIDIA H20 GPUs and\na global batch size of 4,096 acoustic-patch frames. We use\nAdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter 2019) with bfloat16 pre-\ncision. The learning rate is linearly increased to 1 × 10−4\nover the first 5K updates and then decayed with a cosine\nschedule. The flow-matching and stop-prediction losses are\nassigned unit weights, and the semantic anchoring weight is\nset to λsem = 0.1. Inference uses 10 function evaluations\n(NFE = 10), a classifier-free guidance scale of 2.0 (Ho and\nSalimans 2022), and a sampling temperature of 1.0.\nEvaluation Metrics.\nWe evaluate zero-shot TTS on Seed-\nTTS-Eval (Anastassiou et al. 2024) and CV3-Eval (Du et al.\n2025). Content fidelity is measured by character error rate\n(CER) for Chinese using Paraformer (Gao et al. 2022) and\nword error rate (WER) for English using Whisper-large-v3\n(Radford et al. 2023). Speaker similarity (SIM) is computed\nas the cosine similarity between WavLM-based speaker em-\nbeddings\n(Chen et al. 2022), and UTMOS (Saeki et al.\n2022) is used as an automatic estimate of perceptual qual-\nity. We evaluate SA-VAE reconstruction using PESQ (Rix\net al. 2001), STOI (Taal et al. 2011), and UTMOS. For SVS,\nwe follow the GMO-SVS evaluation protocol (Qian et al.\n2026) and report CER/WER, SIM, F0 frame error (FFE),\nSingMOS-Pro (Tang et al. 2025), and Sheet scores.\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nModel\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nCER ↓SIM ↑WER ↓SIM ↑CER ↓SIM ↑UTMOS ↑\nContinuous-AR Baselines\nVoxCPM\n1.52\n0.743\n2.46\n0.675 14.29 0.704\n2.651\n+SA-VAE\n1.63\n0.746\n2.22\n0.683 13.72 0.704\n2.647\nSemBridge Alignment–Anchoring\n-Align,-Anchor\n1.58\n0.745\n2.43\n0.685 16.87 0.696\n2.622\n+Align\n1.51\n0.735\n2.30\n0.678 15.94 0.697\n2.620\n+Anchor\n1.21\n0.742\n2.18\n0.683 13.97 0.700\n2.659\n+Align,+Anchor 1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nAnchoring Depth λsem = 0.1\nNo Anchor\n1.51\n0.735\n2.30\n0.678 15.94 0.697\n2.620\nAnchor@18\n1.13\n0.747\n2.12\n0.688 12.68 0.699\n2.656\nAnchor@24\n1.04\n0.747\n1.93\n0.693 12.01 0.704\n2.683\nAnchor@32\n1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nAnchoring Strength Anchor@32\nλsem = 1.0\n1.64\n0.681\n5.11\n0.591 24.31 0.632\n2.588\nλsem = 0.5\n1.61\n0.705\n3.64\n0.623 16.25 0.663\n2.614\nλsem = 0.1\n1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nλsem = 0.05\n1.13\n0.746\n2.32\n0.683 13.86 0.700\n2.652\nTable 2: Ablation study of target-space semantic alignment\nand predictor-state semantic anchoring. All variants use the\nsame 0.8B backbone, 100K hours of training data, and 300K\nupdates. The default SemBridge configuration uses SA-VAE\nwith semantic alignment and Anchor@32. UTMOS is re-\nported on ZH-Hard for perceptual quality. Bold and under-\nlined values indicate the globally best and second-best re-\nsults, respectively. Tied results receive the same formatting.\nBaselines.\nFor zero-shot TTS, we compare SemBridge\nwith representative systems from several speech genera-\ntion paradigms. Non-autoregressive baselines include F5-\nTTS (Chen et al. 2024) and MaskGCT (Wang et al. 2024),\nwhich use flow-matching and masked-generation formula-\ntions, respectively. Discrete-token autoregressive systems in-\nclude the CosyVoice series (Du et al. 2024a,b, 2025), Spark-\nTTS\n(Wang et al. 2025), FireRedTTS (Guo et al. 2024),\nHiggsAudio-v2(Boson AI 2026),and IndexTTS2 (Zhou et al.\n2025a). We also compare with continuous-latent or hybrid\nautoregressive systems, including DiTAR (Jia et al. 2025),\nVoxCPM (Zhou et al. 2025b), VoxCPM2 (Zhou et al. 2026),\nVibeVoice (Peng et al. 2026), MELA-TTS (An et al. 2026),\nand SemaVoice (Wang et al. 2026).\nFor score-conditioned svs, we compare with representative\nrecent systems on GMO-SVS, including StyleSinger (Zhang\net al. 2024b), TCSinger (Zhang et al. 2024c), Vevo (Zhang\net al. 2025), Ying-Music-Singer (Zheng et al. 2025), and\nSoulX-Singer (Qian et al. 2026). Baseline results are taken\nfrom the original publications or obtained using official\ncheckpoints when available. The supplementary material re-\nports the model scale, trai", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false, "persistedOutputPath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa/tool-results/b3fazv1uw.txt", "persistedOutputSize": 67111}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1786345238521", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb369a5911d87679", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa/tool-results/b3fazv1uw.txt"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa", "uuid": "eeb06977-7131-4539-83e8-5be7fecf7702"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb369a5911d87679", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tLooking in indexes: http://nexus.sii.shaipower.online/repository/pypi/simple\n2\tRequirement already satisfied: pymupdf in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (1.27.2.3)\n3\t=== PAGE 1 ===\n4\tSemBridge: Semantic Token Anchoring for Continuous-Latent\n5\tAutoregressive Speech Generation\n6\tHanke Xie1,2∗†, Haopeng Lin2†, Jiale Qian2, Dake Guo1, Yuepeng Jiang1, Zhichao Wang2, Wenxiao\n7\tCao2, Jingbin Hu1, Guobin Ma1, Wenhao Li1, Huakang Chen1, Chengyou Wang1, Ming Tao2,\n8\tZhonghua Fu1, Lei Xie1‡, Xinsheng Wang2‡\n9\t1Audio, Speech and Language Processing Group (ASLP@NPU), School of Software\n10\tNorthwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, China\n11\t2Soul AI Lab, China\n12\thkxie@mail.nwpu.edu.cn, lxie@nwpu.edu.cn, wangxinsheng@soulapp.cn\n13\tAbstract\n14\tContinuous-latent\n15\tautoregressive\n16\tspeech\n17\tgeneration\n18\thas\n19\temerged as a promising alternative to discrete-token modeling\n20\tby avoiding quantization loss and preserving richer acoustic\n21\tinformation. However, continuous acoustic targets do not ex-\n22\tpose linguistic structure as explicit token-level prediction tar-\n23\tgets. Consequently, the autoregressive language model (LM)\n24\tmust acquire linguistic structure indirectly through acous-\n25\ttic prediction, which can compromise the content fidelity of\n26\tgenerated speech. We propose SemBridge, a training-only\n27\tsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\n28\tautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge uses discrete se-\n29\tmantic tokens to directly supervise autoregressive LM states\n30\tand employs a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE to organize\n31\tthe continuous target space under the same semantic refer-\n32\tence. The semantic supervision is used only during train-\n33\ting, while inference remains entirely continuous. We evalu-\n34\tate SemBridge on zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) and score-\n35\tconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS). Across multi-\n36\tple benchmarks, SemBridge improves content accuracy, as\n37\tmeasured by word and character error rates (WER/CER),\n38\twhile maintaining competitive speaker similarity and percep-\n39\ttual quality. Experimental results demonstrate that explicit\n40\tsemantic-token supervision for autoregressive state learning\n41\tis an effective and general direction for continuous speech\n42\tgeneration. Speech samples are available.1 The model code\n43\tand checkpoints will be available at https://github.com/ASLP-\n44\tlab/SemBridge.\n45\tIntroduction\n46\tAutoregressive (AR) models have emerged as a dominant\n47\tparadigm for speech synthesis. (Wang et al. 2023; Anas-\n48\ttassiou et al. 2024). Most AR speech generation systems\n49\tadopt a language-modeling formulation, representing speech\n50\tas discrete token sequences and formulating generation as\n51\tstandard next-token prediction. Recent AR systems fur-\n52\tther advance discrete speech representations by introducing\n53\t1https://tiamojames.github.io/SemBridge_Demo/\n54\t∗Work done during an internship at Soul AI Lab.\n55\t†These authors contributed equally.\n56\t‡Corresponding author.\n57\tlow-rate and semantically structured speech tokens through\n58\tpretrained speech representations or hierarchical semantic–\n59\tacoustic modeling (Du et al. 2024b, 2025; Hu et al. 2026;\n60\tZhang et al. 2024a). These designs provide compact and lin-\n61\tguistically meaningful prediction targets, reducing the text–\n62\tspeech semantic gap and improving autoregressive model-\n63\ting. However, discrete representations inevitably introduce\n64\tquantization constraints, limiting their capacity to preserve\n65\tfine-grained acoustic details required for high-fidelity speech\n66\tgeneration. Continuous representations avoid quantization\n67\tloss while providing a richer and more expressive acoustic\n68\ttarget space. Consequently, continuous-latent autoregressive\n69\tgeneration has emerged as a promising direction for high-\n70\tfidelity speech synthesis (Meng et al. 2025; Jia et al. 2025;\n71\tXia et al. 2026).\n72\tContinuous-latent autoregressive models generate speech\n73\tby predicting continuous acoustic representations, such as\n74\tmel-spectrograms or learned acoustic latents, often using\n75\tdiffusion- or flow-based next-patch generation to model com-\n76\tplex acoustic distributions\n77\t(Jia et al. 2025; Peng et al.\n78\t2026; Zhou et al. 2025b). These continuous targets preserve\n79\trich acoustic information while implicitly encoding linguis-\n80\ttic content. Unlike discrete semantic tokens, however, they\n81\tdo not provide explicit semantic targets at each generation\n82\tstep. Consequently, the autoregressive LM is required to infer\n83\tlinguistic structure indirectly from the continuous generation\n84\tobjective, without explicit token-level semantic supervision.\n85\tRecent studies have explored various strategies for in-\n86\tcorporating semantic information into continuous speech\n87\tgeneration. Existing approaches mainly leverage continuous\n88\tsemantic representations extracted from pretrained speech\n89\tmodels to enhance either target representations or autore-\n90\tgressive hidden states (Wang et al. 2026; An et al. 2026).\n91\tWhile these methods demonstrate the importance of seman-\n92\ttic modeling for continuous generation, they formulate se-\n93\tmantic supervision as continuous feature alignment. In con-\n94\ttrast, discrete semantic tokens organize linguistic content into\n95\tdiscrete semantic units and provide explicit prediction tar-\n96\tgets for autoregressive language modeling. Such discretiza-\n97\ttion makes clusterable linguistic structure explicit while sup-\n98\tpressing linguistically irrelevant variation retained in con-\n99\tarXiv:2608.07462v1  [eess.AS]  7 Aug 2026\n100\t\n101\t=== PAGE 2 ===\n102\ttinuous speech features (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen, Sagot,\n103\tand Dupoux 2022). However, leveraging discrete semantic\n104\ttokens to explicitly supervise continuous-latent autoregres-\n105\tsive states remains underexplored. Other approaches intro-\n106\tduce hierarchical or semi-discrete architectures to combine\n107\tsemantic and acoustic modeling (Zhou et al. 2025b; Yu et al.\n108\t2025); however, they modify the original continuous genera-\n109\ttion interface by introducing additional semantic generation\n110\tcomponents.\n111\tTo address this gap, we introduce SemBridge, a training-\n112\tonly semantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-\n113\tlatent autoregressive speech generation. At its core, Sem-\n114\tBridge uses discrete semantic-token labels to provide direct\n115\tsemantic supervision for autoregressive LM states, while\n116\tcontinuous latent patches remain the sole generated and\n117\trecurrent variables. A complementary Semantic-Aligned\n118\tAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE) further aligns continuous acoustic\n119\tlatents with token-level embeddings from the same semantic\n120\ttokenizer, establishing a shared semantic reference for latent\n121\trepresentation learning and state supervision.\n122\tWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot text-to-\n123\tspeech (TTS) and further assess its transferability to score-\n124\tconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS) (Liu et al. 2022;\n125\tZhang et al. 2022; Qian et al. 2026). Across multiple\n126\tTTS and SVS benchmarks, SemBridge consistently reduces\n127\tWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker similar-\n128\tity and perceptual quality. Controlled ablations show that\n129\tsemantic-token anchoring provides complementary gains be-\n130\tyond target-space alignment alone, while layer-wise analyses\n131\treveal a trade-off between content accuracy and synthesis\n132\tquality.\n133\tOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n134\t• We propose SemBridge, a training-only semantic-token\n135\tanchoring framework that directly supervises autoregres-\n136\tsive LM states with discrete semantic-token labels while\n137\tpreserving continuous-only generation during inference.\n138\t• We introduce a unified semantic supervision scheme\n139\tbased on a shared frozen semantic tokenizer: token-level\n140\tembeddings align continuous acoustic latents through\n141\tSA-VAE, while discrete semantic-token labels explicitly\n142\tsupervise autoregressive LM hidden states.\n143\t• Experiments on zero-shot TTS and score-conditioned\n144\tSVS\n145\tshow\n146\tthat\n147\tSemBridge\n148\tconsistently\n149\timproves\n150\tWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker sim-\n151\tilarity and perceptual quality. Ablations further confirm\n152\tthe complementary benefits of latent alignment and state\n153\tanchoring and reveal a trade-off effect of anchoring depth.\n154\tRelated Work\n155\tDiscrete-Token\n156\tAutoregressive\n157\tSpeech\n158\tGeneration.\n159\tModern LM-based speech systems commonly represent\n160\tspeech as discrete token sequences produced by semantic\n161\tor neural-codec tokenizers. AudioLM, VALL-E, and Seed-\n162\tTTS formulate speech generation as autoregressive sequence\n163\tmodeling over discrete speech representations, enabling scal-\n164\table generation and zero-shot adaptation (Borsos et al. 2022;\n165\tWang et al. 2023; Anastassiou et al. 2024). Recent work\n166\tincreasingly explores low-rate and semantically informed to-\n167\tkens to shorten speech sequences and strengthen linguistic\n168\tmodeling. SpeechTokenizer distributes semantic and acous-\n169\ttic information across multiple quantization levels, while\n170\tWavTokenizer explores a compact single-codebook repre-\n171\tsentation (Zhang et al. 2024a; Ji et al. 2025). Self-supervised\n172\tspeech studies further show that learned representations ex-\n173\thibit clusterable phonetic structure, while discrete-unit analy-\n174\tses demonstrate that discretization can suppress linguistically\n175\tirrelevant variation and provide effective prediction targets\n176\tfor spoken language modeling (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen,\n177\tSagot, and Dupoux 2022). Nevertheless, quantization intro-\n178\tduces an information bottleneck that can limit the preserva-\n179\ttion of fine-grained acoustic details.\n180\tContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Speech Generation.\n181\tContinuous-latent approaches avoid discrete quantization by\n182\tdirectly modeling mel-spectrograms, learned acoustic la-\n183\ttents, or latent distributions. MELLE autoregressively gen-\n184\terates continuous mel-spectrogram frames, while KALL-E\n185\tmodels the distribution of future continuous latents (Meng\n186\tet al. 2025; Xia et al. 2026). Recent methods further combine\n187\tcausal sequence modeling with diffusion- or flow-based local\n188\tgeneration. DiTAR separates inter-patch dependency model-\n189\ting from intra-patch acoustic generation through a causal\n190\tLM and a local diffusion transformer (Jia et al. 2025), while\n191\tVibeVoiceuseslow-rate continuousrepresentationsandnext-\n192\tpatch diffusion for long-form multi-speaker generation (Peng\n193\tet al. 2026). These approaches preserve an expressive contin-\n194\tuous acoustic target space, but their autoregressive states are\n195\ttrained primarily through continuous generation objectives.\n196\tSemantic Guidance for Continuous Autoregressive Gen-\n197\teration.\n198\tRecent methods introduce semantic guidance into\n199\tcontinuous generation through pretrained speech representa-\n200\ttions. SemaVoice aligns continuous VAE latents with high-\n201\tlevel features from a frozen speech foundation model, im-\n202\tproving semantic structure in the acoustic target space be-\n203\tfore autoregressive training (Wang et al. 2026). MELA-TTS\n204\tinstead regularizes autoregressive states by aligning them\n205\twith continuous ASR features (An et al. 2026). These meth-\n206\tods transfer semantic information through continuous feature\n207\talignment at the target and predictor levels, respectively. Vox-\n208\tCPM adopts a semi-discrete semantic-prosodic bottleneck\n209\ttogether with a residual pathway, retaining an explicit seman-\n210\ttic modeling branch during inference (Zhou et al. 2025b).\n211\tMethod\n212\tOverview\n213\tSemBridge is built on a continuous-latent autoregressive\n214\tbackbone that generates acoustic latent patches conditioned\n215\ton symbolic inputs and preceding acoustic history. Its core\n216\tmechanism is semantic-token anchoring, which uses dis-\n217\tcrete semantic-token labels to provide direct supervision\n218\tfor selected autoregressive LM states. A complementary\n219\tSemantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) organizes the\n220\tcontinuous acoustic target space under the same semantic\n221\treference. Together, the two objectives introduce semantic\n222\tstructure into both the predictive LM states and the contin-\n223\t\n224\t=== PAGE 3 ===\n225\tSemantic   Anchoring\n226\tSemantic\n227\tEncoder\n228\tAcoustic\n229\tEncoder\n230\tVariational\n231\tPosterior\n232\tVQ\n233\tSemantic Token\n234\tAlign Loss\n235\tAcoustic \n236\tReconstruction\n237\tLoss\n238\tAcoustic\n239\tDecoder\n240\tSemantic\n241\tProjector\n242\tDecoder-Only Transformer\n243\tUnified Prefix\n244\tTask\n245\tText\n246\tMiDi\n247\tPatch Enc\n248\tPatch Enc\n249\t(A) Semantic-Aligned Acoustic Latent Tokenizer\n250\t(B) SemBridge: Next Acoustic-Latent Prediction \n251\tLocDiT\n252\tLocDiT\n253\tLocDiT\n254\tSemantic Feature\n255\tAcoustic Feature\n256\tSemantic Token\n257\tAcoustic Latent\n258\tPatch Enc\n259\tPatch Latent \n260\tText Sequence\n261\tText Embedding\n262\tTraining only\n263\tTraining & Inference\n264\tCe Loss \n265\t \n266\tSemantic Head\n267\tHidden State \n268\tInference only\n269\tFigure 1: Overview of the two-stage SemBridge framework. (A) Stage I trains SA-VAE to reconstruct waveforms while aligning\n270\tcontinuous acoustic patches with embeddings from a frozen semantic tokenizer; their token IDs are retained as Stage-II targets.\n271\t(B) Stage II trains a continuous-latent autoregressive generator: a causal LM encodes symbolic conditions and preceding acoustic\n272\tpatches, LocDiT predicts the next patch, and a semantic head supervises a LM hidden layer under the same causal shift.\n273\tuous targets they model, while retaining continuous latent\n274\tpatches as the sole generated and recurrent variables.\n275\tWe use an open-source pretrained GLM-4 tokenizer(Zeng\n276\tet al. 2024) operating at 12.5 Hz. Given a training waveform\n277\tx, the tokenizer produces a sequence of token-level semantic\n278\tembeddings and their corresponding discrete token IDs:\n279\t(E, S) = SGLM(x), E = {et}T\n280\tt=1, S = {st}T\n281\tt=1,\n282\t(1)\n283\twhere et denotes the continuous embedding associated with\n284\tthe semantic token st, and st ∈{1, . . . , V } with vocabu-\n285\tlary size V = 16,384. The embeddings and token IDs pro-\n286\tvide two complementary forms of supervision: et is used for\n287\tcontinuous target-space alignment, whereas st serves as the\n288\tcategorical target for LM-state anchoring.\n289\tThe SA-VAE encoder produces frame-level continuous\n290\tacoustic latents A = {an}2T\n291\tn=1 at 25 Hz. We group every two\n292\tconsecutive frames into one autoregressive acoustic patch:\n293\tzt = Concat (a2t−1, a2t) ,\n294\tt = 1, . . . , T.\n295\t(2)\n296\tThe resulting acoustic patch sequence Z = {zt}T\n297\tt=1 therefore\n298\thas the same 12.5-Hz rate as the semantic tokenizer outputs.\n299\tThis establishes a one-to-one temporal correspondence:\n300\tzt ←→(et, st) .\n301\t(3)\n302\tAccordingly, each continuous patch is aligned with a token-\n303\tlevel embedding during SA-VAE training, and the LM state\n304\tresponsible for predicting that patch is supervised by the\n305\tcorresponding discrete token ID during generator training.\n306\tTraining proceeds in two stages as shown in Figure 1.\n307\tStage I trains SA-VAE with acoustic reconstruction and se-\n308\tmantic target-alignment objectives. Stage II freezes SA-VAE\n309\tand the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer, and trains the continuous\n310\tautoregressive generator with its original generation objec-\n311\ttives together with semantic-token anchoring. Although SA-\n312\tVAE is trained first, the defining component of SemBridge\n313\tis the Stage-II token-level supervision applied directly to au-\n314\ttoregressive LM states.\n315\tContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Backbone\n316\tGiven the continuous acoustic patch sequence Z\n317\t=\n318\t{z1, . . . , zT } produced by the frozen SA-VAE, SemBridge\n319\tautoregressively models speech generation conditioned on a\n320\tsymbolic input c and an optional acoustic prompt r:\n321\tp(Z | c, r) =\n322\tT\n323\tY\n324\tt=1\n325\tp (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n326\t(4)\n327\tAutoregressive\n328\tContext\n329\tModeling.\n330\tFollowing\n331\trecent\n332\tcontinuous-latent autoregressive frameworks\n333\t(Jia et al.\n334\t2025), the generator consists of PatchEnc, a causal LM, and\n335\ta local diffusion transformer, denoted as LocDiT. PatchEnc\n336\tmaps the acoustic prompt and preceding continuous patches\n337\tinto acoustic context representations, which are combined\n338\twith the symbolic conditions and processed by the causal\n339\tLM. At the position responsible for predicting zt, the hidden\n340\tstate produced by Transformer layer ℓis:\n341\th(ℓ)\n342\tt\n343\t= H(ℓ)\n344\tθ\n345\t(c, r, z<t) ,\n346\tℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n347\t(5)\n348\tThe causal construction ensures that h(ℓ)\n349\tt\n350\tonly depends on the\n351\tsymbolic conditions, prompt context, and preceding acoustic\n352\tpatches. The final-layer state provides the global condition\n353\tfor continuous next-patch generation, while a selected hid-\n354\tden layer is additionally supervised by the semantic-token\n355\tanchoring objective described in Section Semantic-Token\n356\tAnchoring.\n357\tConditional Continuous Patch Generation.\n358\tThe normal-\n359\tized final LM state\n360\tgt = RMSNorm\n361\t\u0010\n362\th(L)\n363\tt\n364\t\u0011\n365\t(6)\n366\tconditions LocDiT together with the preceding acoustic\n367\tpatch zt−1. Rather than directly regressing zt, LocDiT mod-\n368\tels its conditional distribution using flow matching:\n369\tLFM = E\n370\th\n371\t∥vΘ (ezt,τ, τ; gt, zt−1) −v⋆\n372\tt ∥2\n373\t2\n374\ti\n375\t,\n376\t(7)\n377\t\n378\t=== PAGE 4 ===\n379\twhere τ denotes the flow time, ezt,τ is the corresponding\n380\tintermediate flow state, and v⋆\n381\tt is the target velocity field.\n382\tA lightweight stop predictor attached to the final LM state\n383\tdetermines sequence termination.\n384\tSemantic-Token Anchoring\n385\tCausally Aligned Semantic Prediction.\n386\tLet h(ℓ)\n387\tt\n388\tdenote\n389\tthe post-block hidden state produced by Transformer layer ℓ\n390\tat the autoregressive position responsible for predicting the\n391\tnext continuous patch zt:\n392\th(ℓ)\n393\tt\n394\t= H(ℓ)\n395\tθ\n396\t(c, r, z<t) ,\n397\tℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n398\t(8)\n399\tBecause the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer and the autoregres-\n400\tsive acoustic patches share the same 12.5-Hz temporal rate,\n401\tthe semantic token st is associated with the same target patch\n402\tzt. Under the causal shift used for next-patch prediction, the\n403\tstate h(ℓ)\n404\tt\n405\thas access only to the symbolic conditions, prompt\n406\tcontext, and preceding acoustic patches z<t. It does not ob-\n407\tserve either the target patch zt or its semantic label st.\n408\tThe same autoregressive state is therefore trained to sup-\n409\tport two complementary predictions:\n410\th(L)\n411\tt\n412\t−→zt,\n413\th(ℓ⋆)\n414\tt\n415\t−→st,\n416\t(9)\n417\twhere the final-layer state conditions continuous next-patch\n418\tgeneration, while a selected intermediate or final layer ℓ⋆\n419\tis anchored by the corresponding semantic-token label. Se-\n420\tmantic tokens are used only as supervision targets and never\n421\tenter the autoregressive context.\n422\tDepth-Selective\n423\tToken\n424\tClassification.\n425\tWe\n426\tattach\n427\ta\n428\tlightweight semantic prediction head fϕ to the post-block\n429\thidden state at a selected Transformer depth ℓ⋆. The condi-\n430\ttional semantic-token distribution is:\n431\tqϕ\n432\t\u0010\n433\tst | h(ℓ⋆)\n434\tt\n435\t\u0011\n436\t= softmax\n437\t\u0010\n438\tfϕ\n439\t\u0010\n440\th(ℓ⋆)\n441\tt\n442\t\u0011\u0011\n443\t.\n444\t(10)\n445\tUnlike continuous feature alignment, this objective does not\n446\trequire the autoregressive state to reproduce the full geome-\n447\ttry of a teacher representation. Instead, it assigns an explicit\n448\tsemantic classification target to each state responsible for\n449\tcontinuous next-patch prediction.\n450\tThe anchoring objective is computed over all valid acous-\n451\ttic prediction positions:\n452\tLsem = −1\n453\t|T |\n454\tX\n455\tt∈T\n456\tlog qϕ\n457\t\u0010\n458\tst | h(ℓ⋆)\n459\tt\n460\t\u0011\n461\t,\n462\t(11)\n463\twhere T denotes the set of valid autoregressive acoustic\n464\tpositions after excluding padded positions. We allow the at-\n465\ttachment depth ℓ⋆to vary and study its effect experimentally.\n466\tJoint\n467\tContinuous\n468\tand\n469\tSemantic\n470\tObjectives.\n471\tThe\n472\tsemantic-token objective is jointly optimized with the origi-\n473\tnal continuous-latent generation losses:\n474\tLgen = LFM + λsemLsem + λstopLstop.\n475\t(12)\n476\tThe flow-matching objective trains continuous next-patch\n477\tgeneration, while Lsem updates the semantic head and the\n478\tautoregressive LM states. The SA-VAE and GLM-4 semantic\n479\ttokenizer remain frozen throughout Stage II. Consequently,\n480\tthe semantic objective acts as a state-level training constraint\n481\twithout changing the acoustic target or introducing discrete\n482\tvariables into the recurrent generation history.\n483\tContinuous-Only\n484\tInference.\n485\tAt\n486\tinference,\n487\tneither\n488\tsemantic-token labels nor the semantic prediction head\n489\tare required. Generation follows the original continuous\n490\tPatchEnc–LM–LocDiT pathway:\n491\tp(Z | c, r) =\n492\tT\n493\tY\n494\tt=1\n495\tp (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n496\t(13)\n497\tContinuous latent patches remain the only generated vari-\n498\tables and recurrent acoustic history. The semantic-token\n499\tbranch therefore changes how the autoregressive states are\n500\tlearned, rather than how speech is generated.\n501\tTarget-Space Alignment with SA-VAE\n502\tComplementary to state-level semantic-token anchoring, we\n503\tintroduce a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) to\n504\timprove the semantic correspondence of the continuous\n505\tacoustic target space. SA-VAE remains a continuous Gaus-\n506\tsian VAE and uses a frozen semantic tokenizer as its se-\n507\tmantic reference. Given an input waveform x, the tokenizer\n508\textracts token-level semantic embeddings e = Sfeat(x). A\n509\tlightweight projection maps the continuous acoustic latent\n510\trepresentations into the semantic feature space, and seman-\n511\ttic correspondence is optimized through:\n512\tLalign = Dsem (Pz(z), sg [e]) ,\n513\t(14)\n514\twhere Pzdenote lightweight alignment mappings, sg(·) stops\n515\tgradients through the frozen semantic tokenizer, and Dsem\n516\tcombines cosine alignment with a weak L1 constraint.\n517\tThe complete Stage-I objective is:\n518\tLSA-VAE = LVAE + λalignLalign.\n519\t(15)\n520\tThis objective encourages patch-level semantic correspon-\n521\tdence without imposing a discrete bottleneck on the acoustic\n522\trepresentation. After Stage I training, SA-VAE is frozen and\n523\tprovides continuous acoustic targets for generator training.\n524\tTarget-space alignment and state-level anchoring use dif-\n525\tferent forms of supervision from the same semantic tok-\n526\tenizer. SA-VAE aligns zt with the continuous embedding\n527\tet, whereas semantic-token anchoring uses the correspond-\n528\ting discrete ID st to supervise the LM state responsible for\n529\tpredicting zt. Their shared semantic identity and temporal\n530\tcorrespondence make the two objectives complementary.\n531\tTask Conditioning for TTS and SVS\n532\tSemantic-token anchoring is applied to the autoregressive\n533\tLM states responsible for continuous next-patch prediction\n534\tand is shared across TTS and SVS. TTS uses text and lan-\n535\tguage conditions, whereas score-conditioned SVS addition-\n536\tally incorporates lyrics and score-derived pitch and duration;\n537\tboth tasks otherwise share the same continuous acoustic rep-\n538\tresentation, PatchEnc–LM–LocDiT backbone, and anchor-\n539\ting objective. SVS therefore provides a more demanding\n540\textension for examining whether semantic-token supervi-\n541\tsion improves lyric intelligibility under explicit musical con-\n542\tstraints without sacrificing the fine-grained acoustic variation\n543\tand expressiveness of singing.\n544\t\n545\t=== PAGE 5 ===\n546\tModel\n547\tParams Data (h)\n548\tContent Consistency ↓| Speaker Similarity ↑\n549\tSeed-TTS-Eval\n550\tCV3-EVAL\n551\tZH\n552\tEN\n553\tZH-Hard\n554\tZH\n555\tEN\n556\tZH-Hard\n557\tEN-Hard\n558\tReported and open-source systems\n559\tF5-TTS\n560\t0.3B\n561\t100k\n562\t1.53 | 0.760\n563\t2.00 | 0.670\n564\t8.67 | 0.713\n565\t5.47 | –\n566\t8.90 | –\n567\t– | –\n568\t– | –\n569\tCosyVoice2\n570\t0.5B\n571\t170k\n572\t1.38 | 0.757\n573\t3.09 | 0.659\n574\t6.83 | 0.724\n575\t4.08 | –\n576\t6.32 | –\n577\t12.58 | 0.726\n578\t11.96 | 0.667\n579\tCosyVoice3-1.5B\n580\t1.5B\n581\t1.0M\n582\t1.12 | 0.781\n583\t2.22 | 0.720\n584\t5.83 | 0.758\n585\t3.91 | –\n586\t4.99 | –\n587\t9.77 | 0.785\n588\t10.55 | 0.761\n589\tIndexTTS2\n590\t1.5B\n591\t–\n592\t1.03 | 0.765\n593\t2.23 | 0.706\n594\t7.12 | 0.755\n595\t– | –\n596\t– | –\n597\t– | –\n598\t– | –\n599\tHiggsAudio-v2\n600\t3B\n601\t10M\n602\t1.50 | 0.740\n603\t2.44 | 0.677\n604\t55.07 | 0.656\n605\t3.89 | –\n606\t5.24 | –\n607\t14.14 | 0.786\n608\t9.04 | 0.759\n609\tContinuous-latent autoregressive systems\n610\tDiTAR\n611\t0.6B\n612\t–\n613\t1.02 | 0.753\n614\t1.69 | 0.735\n615\t– | –\n616\t– | –\n617\t– | –\n618\t– | –\n619\t– | –\n620\tMELA-TTS\n621\t0.8B\n622\t170k\n623\t0.95 | 0.720\n624\t2.45 | 0.590\n625\t7.75 | 0.710\n626\t– | –\n627\t– | –\n628\t– | –\n629\t– | –\n630\tVoxCPM-Emilia\n631\t0.6B\n632\t100k\n633\t1.11 | 0.740\n634\t2.34 | 0.681\n635\t12.46 | 0.698\n636\t4.47 | –\n637\t5.23 | –\n638\t22.20 | 0.626\n639\t10.00 | 0.626\n640\tVoxCPM\n641\t0.6B\n642\t1.8M\n643\t0.93 | 0.772\n644\t1.85 | 0.729\n645\t8.87 | 0.730\n646\t3.40 | –\n647\t4.04 | –\n648\t12.90 | 0.661\n649\t7.89 | 0.643\n650\tSemaVoice\n651\t1.5B\n652\t100k\n653\t1.32 | 0.728\n654\t1.91 | 0.657\n655\t9.37 | 0.687\n656\t– | –\n657\t– | –\n658\t– | –\n659\t– | –\n660\tVibeVoice∗\n661\t1.5B\n662\t–\n663\t2.78 | 0.686\n664\t5.28 | 0.588\n665\t29.43 | 0.583\n666\t5.16 | 0.702\n667\t6.96 | 0.560\n668\t15.94 | 0.637\n669\t7.89 | 0.586\n670\tVoxCPM2\n671\t2B\n672\t2M\n673\t0.97 | 0.795\n674\t1.84 | 0.753\n675\t8.13 | 0.753\n676\t3.65 | –\n677\t5.00 | –\n678\t8.55 | –\n679\t8.48 | –\n680\tSemBridge\n681\t0.8B\n682\t120k\n683\t0.95 | 0.758\n684\t1.81 | 0.699\n685\t9.79 | 0.717\n686\t3.34 | 0.757\n687\t4.22 | 0.658\n688\t10.58 | 0.717\n689\t6.35 | 0.619\n690\tTable 1: Objective evaluation on Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL. Each result is reported as content consistency error | speaker\n691\tsimilarity, where Chinese subsets use CER, English subsets use WER, and speaker similarity is measured by SIM. Lower\n692\tcontent error and higher SIM indicate better performance. “–” denotes unavailable results. Bold and underlined values indicate\n693\tthe best and second-best results, respectively. Tied second-best results are both underlined. ∗denotes results obtained through\n694\tour evaluation.\n695\tExperiments\n696\tExperimental Setup\n697\tDatasets.\n698\tWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot\n699\ttext-to-speech (TTS), with score-conditioned singing voice\n700\tsynthesis (SVS) as a cross-task extension. SA-VAE is trained\n701\tseparately on 25K hours of audio, including 20K hours of\n702\tspeech sampled from public corpora and a 5K-hour subset\n703\tof our internal singing data. All matched TTS models and\n704\tcomponent ablations are trained on the 100K-hour bilingual\n705\topen-source VoxBox corpus (Wang et al. 2025). For joint\n706\tTTS and SVS training, we augment VoxBox with the full\n707\t20K hours of internal singing data, resulting in a 120K-hour\n708\tmultitask corpus. We use the 100K-hour models for con-\n709\ttrolled component attribution and the 120K-hour model for\n710\tsystem-level comparison and SVS evaluation. Further details\n711\ton corpus composition and preprocessing are provided in the\n712\tsupplementary material.\n713\tModel Architecture.\n714\tSemBridge consists of a continuous-\n715\tlatent autoregressive backbone, a frozen Semantic-Aligned\n716\tAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE), and training-time semantic super-\n717\tvision modules. The causal LM contains 32 Transformer\n718\tblocks with a hidden size of 1,024 and 16 attention heads.\n719\tPatchEnc and LocDiT each contain 8 blocks with the same\n720\thidden size and number of heads. SA-VAE is a causal con-\n721\tvolutional Gaussian VAE that encodes 44.1 kHz waveforms\n722\tinto 64-dimensional continuous latent frames at 25 Hz with-\n723\tout vector quantization. The frozen semantic tokenizer op-\n724\terates at 12.5 Hz with a vocabulary of 16,384 tokens. Dur-\n725\ting Stage I, a lightweight projection aligns SA-VAE acous-\n726\ttic patches with token-level semantic embeddings. During\n727\tStage II, the corresponding discrete token labels supervise\n728\tautoregressive LM states. We denote a semantic head at-\n729\ttached after Transformer block k as Anchor@k.\n730\tExcluding the frozen SA-VAE, the continuous generation\n731\tbackbone contains 800.15M trainable parameters. SA-VAE\n732\tcontains 86.48M parameters, while the alignment projec-\n733\ttion and semantic anchoring module add 2.43M and 16.79M\n734\tparameters, respectively. The alignment projection and se-\n735\tmantic head are used only during training and are removed\n736\tat inference.\n737\tTraining Details.\n738\tWe train SA-VAE independently for\n739\t300K updates on 3.0-second audio segments with a batch\n740\tsize of 48 using 8 NVIDIA H20 GPUs. We then train Sem-\n741\tBridge for 300K updates using 16 NVIDIA H20 GPUs and\n742\ta global batch size of 4,096 acoustic-patch frames. We use\n743\tAdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter 2019) with bfloat16 pre-\n744\tcision. The learning rate is linearly increased to 1 × 10−4\n745\tover the first 5K updates and then decayed with a cosine\n746\tschedule. The flow-matching and stop-prediction losses are\n747\tassigned unit weights, and the semantic anchoring weight is\n748\tset to λsem = 0.1. Inference uses 10 function evaluations\n749\t(NFE = 10), a classifier-free guidance scale of 2.0 (Ho and\n750\tSalimans 2022), and a sampling temperature of 1.0.\n751\tEvaluation Metrics.\n752\tWe evaluate zero-shot TTS on Seed-\n753\tTTS-Eval (Anastassiou et al. 2024) and CV3-Eval (Du et al.\n754\t2025). Content fidelity is measured by character error rate\n755\t(CER) for Chinese using Paraformer (Gao et al. 2022) and\n756\tword error rate (WER) for English using Whisper-large-v3\n757\t(Radford et al. 2023). Speaker similarity (SIM) is computed\n758\tas the cosine similarity between WavLM-based speaker em-\n759\tbeddings\n760\t(Chen et al. 2022), and UTMOS (Saeki et al.\n761\t2022) is used as an automatic estimate of perceptual qual-\n762\tity. We evaluate SA-VAE reconstruction using PESQ (Rix\n763\tet al. 2001), STOI (Taal et al. 2011), and UTMOS. For SVS,\n764\twe follow the GMO-SVS evaluation protocol (Qian et al.\n765\t2026) and report CER/WER, SIM, F0 frame error (FFE),\n766\tSingMOS-Pro (Tang et al. 2025), and Sheet scores.\n767\t\n768\t=== PAGE 6 ===\n769\tModel\n770\tZH\n771\tEN\n772\tZH-Hard\n773\tCER ↓SIM ↑WER ↓SIM ↑CER ↓SIM ↑UTMOS ↑\n774\tContinuous-AR Baselines\n775\tVoxCPM\n776\t1.52\n777\t0.74