2024) and MaskGCT (Wang et al. 2024),\n874\twhich use flow-matching and masked-generation formula-\n875\ttions, respectively. Discrete-token autoregressive systems in-\n876\tclude the CosyVoice series (Du et al. 2024a,b, 2025), Spark-\n877\tTTS\n878\t(Wang et al. 2025), FireRedTTS (Guo et al. 2024),\n879\tHiggsAudio-v2(Boson AI 2026),and IndexTTS2 (Zhou et al.\n880\t2025a). We also compare with continuous-latent or hybrid\n881\tautoregressive systems, including DiTAR (Jia et al. 2025),\n882\tVoxCPM (Zhou et al. 2025b), VoxCPM2 (Zhou et al. 2026),\n883\tVibeVoice (Peng et al. 2026), MELA-TTS (An et al. 2026),\n884\tand SemaVoice (Wang et al. 2026).\n885\tFor score-conditioned svs, we compare with representative\n886\trecent systems on GMO-SVS, including StyleSinger (Zhang\n887\tet al. 2024b), TCSinger (Zhang et al. 2024c), Vevo (Zhang\n888\tet al. 2025), Ying-Music-Singer (Zheng et al. 2025), and\n889\tSoulX-Singer (Qian et al. 2026). Baseline results are taken\n890\tfrom the original publications or obtained using official\n891\tcheckpoints when available. The supplementary material re-\n892\tports the model scale, training data, evaluation source, and\n893\timplementation details for each comparison.\n894\tExperimental Results\n895\tMain Results on Zero-Shot TTS.\n896\tTable 1 reports zero-\n897\tshot TTS results on Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL. The\n898\tsystem-level SemBridge model, trained on the 120K-hour\n899\tmultitask corpus, achieves consistently low content error\n900\tacross languages and evaluation conditions while maintain-\n901\tModel\n902\tRate PESQ STOI UTMOS\n903\tReference Audio\n904\t–\n905\t4.64\n906\t1.00\n907\t4.09\n908\tDiscrete tokenizers\n909\tEncodec\n910\t600\n911\t2.77\n912\t0.94\n913\t3.09\n914\tEncodec\n915\t150\n916\t1.56\n917\t0.85\n918\t1.58\n919\tDAC\n920\t400\n921\t4.01\n922\t0.95\n923\t4.00\n924\tSAC\n925\t37.5\n926\t2.21\n927\t0.90\n928\t4.28\n929\tMimi\n930\t100\n931\t2.26\n932\t0.91\n933\t3.63\n934\tMimi\n935\t200\n936\t2.88\n937\t0.94\n938\t3.87\n939\tWavTokenizer\n940\t40\n941\t1.64\n942\t0.85\n943\t3.03\n944\tX-codec2\n945\t50\n946\t2.43\n947\t0.92\n948\t4.13\n949\tBiCodec\n950\t50\n951\t2.51\n952\t0.92\n953\t4.18\n954\tContinuous tokenizer\n955\tVoxCPM-VAE\n956\t25\n957\t3.23\n958\t0.96\n959\t3.96\n960\tVibeVoice-VAE\n961\t7.5\n962\t2.98\n963\t0.75\n964\t4.18\n965\tSA-VAE\n966\t25\n967\t2.99\n968\t0.96\n969\t3.92\n970\tw/o Alignment\n971\t25\n972\t3.00\n973\t0.96\n974\t3.92\n975\tTable 3: Reconstruction quality of representative discrete\n976\ttokenizers and continuous tokenizer. Rate is measured in\n977\tframes or tokens per second; higher is better for all metrics.\n978\tBold and underlined values indicate the globally best and\n979\tsecond-best results among tokenizers, respectively.\n980\ting competitive speaker similarity. On Seed-TTS-Eval, it ob-\n981\ttains a CER of 0.95 on Chinese, a WER of 1.81 on English,\n982\tand a CER of 9.79 on the Chinese hard subset. On CV3-\n983\tEVAL, it achieves a CER of 3.34 on Chinese, a WER of 4.22\n984\ton English, a CER of 10.58 on the Chinese hard subset, and\n985\ta WER of 6.35 on the English hard subset. Compared with\n986\tMELA-TTS, which achieves competitive content accuracy\n987\tbut lower speaker similarity, particularly on English, Sem-\n988\tBridge provides a more balanced content–speaker trade-off.\n989\tThis pattern is consistent with token-level classification pro-\n990\tviding a more focused semantic regularizer than full contin-\n991\tuous feature matching, though the cross-system comparison\n992\tdoes not establish causality.\n993\tControlled Analysis of Semantic-Token Anchoring\n994\tAcoustic Representation Control.\n995\tTable 2 reports con-\n996\ttrolled experiments using the same 0.8B model scale, 100K\n997\thours of training data, and 300K updates. For the continuous-\n998\tAR baseline, replacing the original acoustic representation\n999\twith SA-VAE reduces English WER from 2.46 to 2.22 and\n1000\tZH-Hard CER from 14.29 to 13.72, while Chinese CER\n1001\tchanges from 1.52 to 1.63. Although gains vary across sub-\n1002\tsets, the results show that acoustic representation affects\n1003\tcontent modeling and motivate the subsequent component\n1004\tanalysis under a fixed SemBridge backbone.\n1005\tAlignment and Anchoring Attribution.\n1006\tWithin the Sem-\n1007\tBridge backbone, the model without target alignment or state\n1008\tanchoring obtains errors of 1.58, 2.43, and 16.87 on ZH, EN,\n1009\tand ZH-Hard, respectively. Target-space alignment alone re-\n1010\tduces these errors to 1.51, 2.30, and 15.94, whereas semantic-\n1011\ttoken anchoring produces larger reductions to 1.21, 2.18, and\n1012\t13.97. Combining the two objectives gives the lowest errors\n1013\tof 1.01, 1.87, and 11.87. These results indicate that state-\n1014\tlevel semantic-token anchoring drives most content-fidelity\n1015\t\n1016\t=== PAGE 7 ===\n1017\tMandarin\n1018\tEnglish\n1019\tSystem\n1020\tCER (%) ↓SIM ↑FFE (%) ↓SingMOS ↑Sheet ↑WER (%) ↓SIM ↑FFE (%) ↓SingMOS ↑Sheet ↑\n1021\tReference and reconstruction bounds\n1022\tReference Audio\n1023\t7.40\n1024\t–\n1025\t–\n1026\t4.624\n1027\t4.334\n1028\t19.70\n1029\t–\n1030\t–\n1031\t4.441\n1032\t3.825\n1033\tExisting SVS systems\n1034\tStyleSinger\n1035\t36.70\n1036\t0.817\n1037\t36.30\n1038\t3.938\n1039\t3.819\n1040\t–\n1041\t–\n1042\t–\n1043\t–\n1044\t–\n1045\tTCSinger\n1046\t27.00\n1047\t0.855\n1048\t31.50\n1049\t3.977\n1050\t3.843\n1051\t41.00\n1052\t0.879\n1053\t21.10\n1054\t3.662\n1055\t3.482\n1056\tVevo\n1057\t30.80\n1058\t0.897\n1059\t11.20\n1060\t4.355\n1061\t4.005\n1062\t48.40\n1063\t0.924\n1064\t8.80\n1065\t4.321\n1066\t3.699\n1067\tYing-Music-Singer\n1068\t9.90\n1069\t0.902\n1070\t13.20\n1071\t4.145\n1072\t3.620\n1073\t–\n1074\t–\n1075\t–\n1076\t–\n1077\t–\n1078\tSoulX-Singer\n1079\t6.90\n1080\t0.905\n1081\t12.20\n1082\t4.445\n1083\t4.107\n1084\t14.90\n1085\t0.926\n1086\t16.40\n1087\t4.303\n1088\t3.705\n1089\tContinuous-latent autoregressive system\n1090\tSemBridge(ours)\n1091\t8.32\n1092\t0.906\n1093\t16.70\n1094\t4.585\n1095\t4.315\n1096\t14.77\n1097\t0.926\n1098\t25.89\n1099\t4.444\n1100\t3.994\n1101\tw/o Anchoring\n1102\t9.18\n1103\t0.905\n1104\t16.74\n1105\t4.560\n1106\t4.306\n1107\t16.29\n1108\t0.926\n1109\t25.97\n1110\t4.432\n1111\t3.984\n1112\tTable 4: Results on GMO-SVS. Lower CER/WER/FFE and higher SIM/SingMOS/Sheet are better. Bold and underlined values\n1113\tindicate the best and second-best synthesized results, respectively; tied results receive the same formatting.\n1114\tgains, while target-space alignment provides additional com-\n1115\tplementary gains.\n1116\tAnchoring Depth.\n1117\tWe vary anchoring depth by attach-\n1118\ting the semantic prediction head after different Transformer\n1119\tblocks. Anchor@32 yields the lowest content errors, with a\n1120\tCER of 1.01 on ZH, a WER of 1.87 on EN, and a CER of\n1121\t11.87 on ZH-Hard. Anchor@24 increases these errors only\n1122\tslightly to 1.04, 1.93, and 12.01, while improving SIM from\n1123\t0.743, 0.687, and 0.697 to 0.747, 0.693, and 0.704, respec-\n1124\ttively. It also raises ZH-Hard UTMOS from 2.648 to 2.683.\n1125\tWe therefore use Anchor@24 for the system-level model be-\n1126\tcause it better balances content accuracy, speaker similarity,\n1127\tand perceptual quality. Overall, anchoring depth trades off\n1128\tcontent and quality metrics.\n1129\tAnchoring Strength.\n1130\tWe vary λsem to assess semantic\n1131\tsupervision strength. Increasing λsem from 0.1 to 0.5 wors-\n1132\tens English WER from 1.87 to 3.64 and reduces SIM from\n1133\t0.687 to 0.623. Setting λsem = 1.0 further degrades content,\n1134\tsimilarity, and UTMOS metrics. A smaller weight of 0.05\n1135\tpreserves speaker similarity and perceptual quality but gives\n1136\thigher content errors than 0.1. We therefore use λsem = 0.1,\n1137\twhich minimizes content errors while maintaining compa-\n1138\trable synthesis quality. These results suggest that seman-\n1139\ttic anchoring should regularize autoregressive state learning\n1140\twithout dominating the continuous generation objective.\n1141\tSA-VAE Reconstruction under Semantic Alignment\n1142\tTa-\n1143\tble 3 compares SA-VAE reconstruction with representative\n1144\tdiscrete and continuous speech representations. At 25 Hz,\n1145\tSA-VAE achieves a PESQ of 2.99, a STOI of 0.96, and a UT-\n1146\tMOS score of 3.92. Its unaligned counterpart obtains nearly\n1147\tidentical scores of 3.00, 0.96, and 3.92, respectively, showing\n1148\tthat semantic alignment does not noticeably degrade acous-\n1149\ttic reconstruction. Despite different representation rates and\n1150\tarchitectures, SA-VAE remains competitive with both con-\n1151\ttinuous and discrete tokenizers. These results support its role\n1152\tas a complementary target-space component that introduces\n1153\tsemantic alignment without imposing a discrete bottleneck\n1154\tor sacrificing reconstruction quality.\n1155\tCross-Task Transfer to Score-Conditioned SVS.\n1156\tTable 4\n1157\tevaluates semantic-token anchoring on score-conditioned\n1158\tSVS. Compared with the model without anchoring, Sem-\n1159\tBridge reduces Mandarin CER from 9.18 to 8.32 and English\n1160\tWER from 16.29 to 14.77, while speaker similarity remains\n1161\tessentially unchanged. SingMOS and Sheet scores also im-\n1162\tprove slightly in both languages, showing that improved lyric\n1163\tintelligibility does not compromise perceived singing qual-\n1164\tity or expressiveness. SemBridge further achieves the highest\n1165\tsynthesized SingMOS and Sheet scores among the compared\n1166\tsystems. Consistent gains across Mandarin and English sug-\n1167\tgest that the semantic constraint remains effective when lin-\n1168\tguistic prediction is coupled with score-derived pitch and\n1169\ttiming conditions. Its FFE results are not the best, indicating\n1170\tthat semantic-token anchoring mainly improves lyric model-\n1171\ting and perceptual quality rather than pitch accuracy. Over-\n1172\tall, the proposed supervision transfers beyond TTS without\n1173\trequiring a task-specific semantic branch.\n1174\tConclusion\n1175\tIn this paper, we introduced SemBridge, a training-only\n1176\tsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\n1177\tautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge directly super-\n1178\tvises autoregressive LM states with discrete semantic-token\n1179\tlabels, while a complementary Semantic-Aligned Acoustic\n1180\tVAE aligns continuous acoustic targets with embeddings\n1181\tfrom the same frozen semantic tokenizer. This shared se-\n1182\tmantic reference strengthens state-level linguistic modeling\n1183\tand target-space semantic correspondence while preserv-\n1184\ting continuous-only autoregressive inference. Experiments\n1185\ton zero-shot TTS and score-conditioned SVS demonstrate\n1186\tthat SemBridge consistently improves content fidelity while\n1187\tmaintaining competitive speaker similarity and perceptual\n1188\tquality. Controlled ablations further confirm the complemen-\n1189\ttary roles of latent alignment and state anchoring and reveal a\n1190\ttrade-off effect of anchoring depth. Overall, semantic-token\n1191\tanchoring provides an effective training strategy for improv-\n1192\ting linguistic modeling in continuous-latent autoregressive\n1193\tspeech generation.\n1194\t\n1195\t=== PAGE 8 ===\n1196\tReferences\n1197\tAn, K.; Zhang, Z.; Gao, C.; Li, Y.; Peng, Z.; Wang, H.; Du,\n1198\tZ.; Zhao, H.; Gao, Z.; and Li, X. 2026. MELA-TTS: Joint\n1199\tTransformer-Diffusion Model with Representation Align-\n1200\tment for Speech Synthesis.\n1201\tIn Proceedings of the IEEE\n1202\tInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n1203\tProcessing.\n1204\tAnastassiou, P.; Chen, J.; Chen, J.; Chen, Y.; Chen, Z.; Chen,\n1205\tZ.; Cong, J.; Deng, L.; Ding, C.; Gao, L.; Gong, M.; Huang,\n1206\tP.; Huang, Q.; Huang, Z.; Huo, Y.; Jia, D.; Li, C.; Li, F.;\n1207\tLi, H.; Li, J.; Li, X.; Li, X.; Liu, L.;