eech tokens. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.01710.\n1348\tWang, Y.; Zhan, H.; Liu, L.; Zeng, R.; Guo, H.; Zheng, J.;\n1349\tZhang, Q.; Zhang, X.; Zhang, S.; and Wu, Z. 2024. Maskgct:\n1350\tZero-shot text-to-speech with masked generative codec trans-\n1351\tformer. arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.00750.\n1352\tXia, K.; Zhu, X.; Yao, J.; Tian, W.; Li, W.; and Xie, L.\n1353\t2026. KALL-E: Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Next-\n1354\tDistribution Prediction. In Proceedings of the AAAI Confer-\n1355\tence on Artificial Intelligence, volume 40, 34016–34024.\n1356\tYu, F.; Wang, T.; Wu, Y.; Zhu, L.; Deng, W.; Han, W.; Wang,\n1357\tW.; Hu, L.; Liang, X.; He, X.; Huang, Y.; Gu, Y.; Liu, Y.;\n1358\tWang, Y.; Xiao, Z.; Wang, Z.; Dong, B.; Dang, F.; Chen, J.;\n1359\tLi, J.; Wang, J.; Jin, Y.; Zhang, Y.; Sheng, Z.; and Wang,\n1360\tX. 2025. JoyVoice: Long-Context Conditioning for Anthro-\n1361\tpomorphic Multi-Speaker Conversational Synthesis. arXiv\n1362\tpreprint arXiv:2512.19090.\n1363\tZeng, A.; Du, Z.; Liu, M.; Wang, K.; Jiang, S.; Zhao, L.;\n1364\tDong, Y.; and Tang, J. 2024. GLM-4-Voice: Towards Intel-\n1365\tligent and Human-Like End-to-End Spoken Chatbot. arXiv\n1366\tpreprint arXiv:2412.02612.\n1367\tZhang, X.; Zhang, D.; Li, S.; Zhou, Y.; and Qiu, X. 2024a.\n1368\tSpeechTokenizer: Unified Speech Tokenizer for Speech Lan-\n1369\tguage Models.\n1370\tIn International Conference on Learning\n1371\tRepresentations.\n1372\tZhang, X.; Zhang, X.; Peng, K.; Tang, Z.; Manohar, V.; Liu,\n1373\tY.; Hwang, J.; Li, D.; Wang, Y.; Chan, J.; Huang, Y.; Wu, Z.;\n1374\tand Ma, M. 2025. Vevo: Controllable Zero-Shot Voice Imita-\n1375\ttion with Self-Supervised Disentanglement. In International\n1376\tConference on Learning Representations. OpenReview.net.\n1377\tZhang, Y.; Cong, J.; Xue, H.; Xie, L.; Zhu, P.; and Bi,\n1378\tM. 2022. VISinger: Variational Inference with Adversarial\n1379\tLearning for End-to-End Singing Voice Synthesis. In Pro-\n1380\tceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n1381\tSpeech and Signal Processing, 7237–7241.\n1382\tZhang, Y.; Huang, R.; Li, R.; He, J.; Xia, Y.; Chen, F.; Duan,\n1383\tX.; Huai, B.; and Zhao, Z. 2024b. StyleSinger: Style Transfer\n1384\tfor Out-of-Domain Singing Voice Synthesis. In Proceedings\n1385\tof the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, volume 38,\n1386\t19597–19605. AAAI Press.\n1387\tZhang, Y.; Jiang, Z.; Li, R.; Pan, C.; He, J.; Huang, R.; Wang,\n1388\tC.; and Zhao, Z. 2024c. TCSinger: Zero-Shot Singing Voice\n1389\tSynthesis with Style Transfer and Multi-Level Style Con-\n1390\ttrol. In Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical\n1391\tMethods in Natural Language Processing, 1960–1975. As-\n1392\tsociation for Computational Linguistics.\n1393\tZheng, J.; Hao, C.; Ma, G.; Zhang, X.; Chen, G.; Ding, C.;\n1394\tChen, Z.; and Xie, L. 2025. YingMusic-Singer: Zero-shot\n1395\tSinging Voice Synthesis and Editing with Annotation-free\n1396\tMelody Guidance. arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.04779.\n1397\tZhou, S.; Zhou, Y.; He, Y.; Zhou, X.; Wang, J.; Deng, W.; and\n1398\tShu, J. 2025a. IndexTTS2: A Breakthrough in Emotionally\n1399\tExpressive and Duration-Controlled Auto-Regressive Zero-\n1400\tShot Text-to-Speech. arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.21619.\n1401\tZhou, Y.; Zeng, G.; Liu, X.; Li, X.; Yu, R.; Gui, J.; Wu, J.;\n1402\tWang, Z.; Shen, X.; Ye, R.; Zhang, Z.; Zhou, J.; Bai, B.; Sun,\n1403\tW.; Deng, M.; Shi, Q.; Wu, Z.; and Liu, Z. 2026. VoxCPM2\n1404\tTechnical Report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.06928.\n1405\tZhou, Y.; Zeng, G.; Liu, X.; Li, X.; Yu, R.; Wang, Z.; Ye,\n1406\tR.; Sun, W.; Gui, J.; Li, K.; Wu, Z.; and Liu, Z. 2025b.\n1407\tVoxCPM: Tokenizer-Free TTS for Context-Aware Speech\n1408\tGeneration and True-to-Life Voice Cloning. arXiv preprint\n1409\tarXiv:2509.24650.\n1410\tTraining Data\n1411\tSA-VAE and the SemBridge are trained in separate stages us-\n1412\ting counted training corpora. SA-VAE is trained on 19,906\n1413\thours of Mandarin and English speech together with a 5,000-\n1414\thour subset of singing data, yielding 24,906 hours of au-\n1415\tdio in total. The matched TTS configurations are trained on\n1416\t100K hours of bilingual VoxBox speech. The Joint TTS–\n1417\tSVS model additionally uses the complete 20K-hour score-\n1418\taligned singing collection, resulting in a 120K-hour cor-\n1419\tpus.Table 5 summarizes the data used at each training stage.\n1420\tSA-VAE Training Data.\n1421\tPanel (a) of Table 5 lists the\n1422\tcomposition of the SA-VAE training corpus. The Mandarin\n1423\tspeech data consist of Emilia-ZH, WenetSpeech4TTS, and\n1424\tin-house paralinguistic speech. The English speech data con-\n1425\tsist of LibriSpeech, the small and medium LibriHeavy sub-\n1426\tsets, Emilia-EN, MLS-EN, and in-house speech. Together,\n1427\t\n1428\t=== PAGE 10 ===\n1429\t(a) SA-VAE training data\n1430\tDataset\n1431\tGroup\n1432\t#Utt. Hours Avg. s\n1433\tEmilia-ZH\n1434\tZH\n1435\t3.0M\n1436\t6,712\n1437\t8.05\n1438\tWenetSpeech4TTS\n1439\tZH\n1440\t2.0M\n1441\t2,754\n1442\t4.96\n1443\tIn-house Data\n1444\tZH\n1445\t0.2M\n1446\t525\n1447\t7.82\n1448\tLibriSpeech\n1449\tEN\n1450\t0.3M\n1451\t961 12.30\n1452\tLibriHeavy\n1453\tEN\n1454\t1.2M\n1455\t5,042 14.85\n1456\tEmilia-EN\n1457\tEN\n1458\t1.0M\n1459\t2,481\n1460\t8.93\n1461\tMLS-EN\n1462\tEN\n1463\t0.3M\n1464\t1,222 14.66\n1465\tIn-house Data\n1466\tEN\n1467\t0.1M\n1468\t209\n1469\t6.42\n1470\tSpeech subtotal\n1471\tZH/EN\n1472\t8.2M 19,906\n1473\t8.78\n1474\tIn-house Singing\n1475\tSinging\n1476\t2.2M\n1477\t5,000\n1478\t8.18\n1479\tSA-VAE total\n1480\tAll\n1481\t10.4M 24,906\n1482\t8.62\n1483\t(b) SemBridge generator training data\n1484\tSetting\n1485\tSpeech pool\n1486\tSinging pool\n1487\tTotal (h)\n1488\tMatched TTS\n1489\tVoxBox (100K h)\n1490\t–\n1491\t100K\n1492\tJoint TTS–SVS VoxBox (100K h)\n1493\tIn-house (20K h)\n1494\t120K\n1495\tTable 5: Training data for SA-VAE and SemBridge.\n1496\tthese sources provide 19,906 hours of bilingual speech, in-\n1497\tcluding 19,172 hours from public corpora and 734 hours\n1498\tfrom in-house data.\n1499\tWe further sample 5,000 hours from the internal singing\n1500\tcorpus. Stage I uses only the waveforms and does not use the\n1501\tcorresponding lyric or score annotations. The complete SA-\n1502\tVAE training set therefore contains 24,906 hours of audio,\n1503\treferred to as approximately 25K hours.\n1504\tSemBridge Training Data.\n1505\tAll controlled TTS experi-\n1506\tments, including the component, anchoring-depth, and loss-\n1507\tweight studies, use the same 100K-hour Mandarin–English\n1508\tVoxBox speech corpus. This setting keeps the generator train-\n1509\ting data fixed across all controlled component comparisons.\n1510\tThe Joint TTS–SVS model augments the same speech\n1511\tcorpus with the complete 20K-hour internal score-aligned\n1512\tsinging collection, resulting in 120K hours of generator train-\n1513\ting data. The 5K-hour singing subset used for SA-VAE train-\n1514\ting is drawn from this 20K-hour collection. Because SA-VAE\n1515\tis trained separately and then frozen, its 24,906-hour corpus\n1516\tis not added to the 120K-hour generator data count. Re-\n1517\tsults obtained with the Joint model are therefore treated as\n1518\tsystem-level comparisons rather than controlled component\n1519\tablations.\n1520\tExperimental Configuration\n1521\tStage I: Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE\n1522\tSA-VAE is a causal convolutional Gaussian VAE that op-\n1523\terates on 44.1-kHz waveforms. The encoder produces 64-\n1524\tdimensional continuous latent frames at 25 Hz without vector\n1525\tquantization. For each training segment, a latent sequence is\n1526\tsampled from the diagonal Gaussian posterior through repa-\n1527\trameterization. The same sample is passed to both the wave-\n1528\tform decoder and the semantic-alignment projection. Every\n1529\ttwo adjacent latent frames are concatenated into a 2 × 64\n1530\tacoustic patch at 12.5 Hz. The resulting patch is projected\n1531\tinto the embedding space of the frozen semantic tokenizer\n1532\tand aligned with the corresponding stop-gradient semantic\n1533\tembedding.\n1534\tThe patch construction and semantic-alignment objective\n1535\tare defined in Eqs. (2), (14), and (15) of the main paper. The\n1536\tcomplete loss objective used in our implementation is\n1537\tLSA-VAE = 10LMR-STFT + 15LMel + 0.01LKL\n1538\t+ Ladv + 1.5Lfeat + 100Lalign.\n1539\t(16)\n1540\tThe alignment loss is averaged over valid acoustic-patch\n1541\tpositions and combines a dimension-normalized ℓ1 dis-\n1542\ttance with softplus(−cos(·, ·)). Gradients are not propa-\n1543\tgated through the semantic embeddings produced by the\n1544\tfrozen tokenizer. In the alignment ablation, only Lalign is\n1545\tremoved; the encoder–decoder architecture and all acoustic\n1546\treconstruction losses remain unchanged.\n1547\tThe product of the encoder downsampling factors is 2 ×\n1548\t3 × 6 × 7 × 7 = 1764, which yields exactly 25 latent frames\n1549\tper second at 44.1 kHz. The 12.5-Hz acoustic-patch and\n1550\tsemantic-token sequences use the same valid-position mask.\n1551\tIncomplete final patches and padded positions are excluded\n1552\tfrom Lalign.\n1553\tThe adversarial objective uses a multi-period discrimina-\n1554\ttor and a multi-scale sub-band CQT discriminator following\n1555\tthe BigVGAN V2 configuration. The adversarial and feature-\n1556\tmatching losses are enabled after a 1,000-update warm-up.\n1557\tSA-VAE is trained for 300K updates on 3-s segments with\n1558\ta global batch size of 48 using eight NVIDIA H20 GPUs.\n1559\tThe trained SA-VAE is frozen in all subsequent SemBridge\n1560\tgenerator experiments.\n1561\tStage II: Continuous-Latent Autoregressive\n1562\tGenerator\n1563\tThe SemBridge generator follows a PatchEnc–LM–LocDiT\n1564\tarchitecture. PatchEnc maps latent patches from the acoustic\n1565\tprompt and autoregressive history to acoustic-context repre-\n1566\tsentations. A 32-block causal LM jointly models these rep-\n1567\tresentations and a task-specific symbolic prefix. The prefix\n1568\tcontains text for TTS and additionally contains lyric, pitch,\n1569\tonset, and duration sequences for score-conditioned SVS. An\n1570\teight-block LocDiT performs next-patch flow matching con-\n1571\tditioned on the normalized final LM state and the preceding\n1572\tSA-VAE latent patch. A two-layer 1024 →1024 →2 MLP\n1573\twith a SiLU activation predicts whether generation should\n1574\tcontinue or terminate.\n1575\tExcluding the frozen SA-VAE, the continuous generation\n1576\tbackbone contains 800.15M trainable parameters. The se-\n1577\tmantic classifier adds 16.79M parameters during training\n1578\tand is removed at inference. SA-VAE contains 86.48M pa-\n1579\trameters, and its 2.43M-parameter alignment projection is\n1580\tused only during Stage I.\n1581\tTraining Configuration\n1582\tSA-VAE is optimized independently and frozen before gen-\n1583\terator training. Unless otherwise stated, all matched Sem-\n1584\tBridge configurations use the same model architecture, op-\n1585\t\n1586\t=== PAGE 11 ===\n1587\tSetting\n1588\tValue\n1589\tSetting\n1590\tValue\n1591\tWaveform rate\n1592\t44.1 kHz\n1593\tFormulation\n1594\tCausal convolutional Gaussian\n1595\tVAE\n1596\tEncoder downsampling factors\n1597\t[2, 3, 6, 7, 7]\n1598\tDecoder upsampling factors\n1599\t[7, 7, 6, 3, 2]\n1600\tHop size\n1601\t1,764 samples\n1602\tAcoustic latent rate\n1603\t25 Hz\n1604\tLatent dimension\n1605\t64\n1606\tPatch shape and rate\n1607\t2 × 64 at 12.5 Hz\n1608\tSemantic vocabulary size\n1609\t16,384\n1610\tSemantic-token rate\n1611\t12.5 Hz\n1612\tSA-VAE parameters\n1613\t86.48M\n1614\tAlignment projection parameters\n1615\t2.43M\n1616\tAcoustic quantization\n1617\tNone\n1618\tGenerator modeling unit\n1619\tContinuous acoustic latent patch\n1620\tTable 6: SA-VAE architecture and semantic-alignment configuration.\n1621\tComponent\n1622\tBlocks/Layers\n1623\tWidth\n1624\tHeads\n1625\tRate\n1626\tStage II\n1627\tRole\n1628\tPatchEnc\n1629\t8\n1630\t1,024\n1631\t16\n1632\t12.5 Hz\n1633\tTrainable\n1634\tAcoustic prompt and history encoding\n1635\tCausal LM\n1636\t32\n1637\t1,024\n1638\t16\n1639\t12.5 Hz\n1640\tTrainable\n1641\tCausal next-patch state modeling\n1642\tLocDiT\n1643\t8\n1644\t1,024\n1645\t16\n1646\t12.5 Hz\n1647\tTrainable\n1648\tFlow-matching patch generation\n1649\tStop predictor\n1650\t2-layer MLP\n1651\t1,024\n1652\t–\n1653\t12.5 Hz\n1654\tTrainable\n1655\tStop/continue classification\n1656\tSemantic head\n1657\tLinear\n1658\t1,024\n1659\t–\n1660\t12.5 Hz\n1661\tTrainable\n1662\t16,384-unit semantic-token classification\n1663\tSA-VAE decoder\n1664\tCausal conv.\n1665\t–\n1666\t–\n1667\t25Hz\n1668\tFrozen\n1669\tWaveform reconstruction\n1670\tTable 7: Architecture of SemBridge.\n1671\ttimization schedule, random seed, and 300K-update training\n1672\tbudget.\n1673\tThe global seed is fixed to 42 for Python, NumPy, PyTorch,\n1674\tand CUDA in all reported experiments.\n1675\tEvaluation Metrics\n1676\tZero-shot TTS.\n1677\tSeed-TTS-Eval contains 2,020 Mandarin\n1678\texamples from DiDiSpeech and 1,088 English examples\n1679\tfrom Common Voice, together with a Mandarin hard subset.\n1680\tFor both Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL, all locally eval-\n1681\tuated systems use the prompt–target pairs, transcripts, and\n1682\tdata partitions provided by the official benchmark manifests.\n1683\tWhen a system returns the acoustic prompt and gener-\n1684\tated continuation as a single waveform, the prompt region\n1685\tis removed before metric computation. Speaker similarity is\n1686\tcomputed between the generated target region and the cor-\n1687\tresponding prompt. The same output-trimming and failure-\n1688\thandling rules are applied to all locally evaluated systems. No\n1689\tutterance is excluded according to its metric value, and results\n1690\tare aggregated separately for each language and benchmark\n1691\tsubset.\n1692\tScore-conditioned SVS.\n1693\tWe evaluate score-conditioned\n1694\tSVS on GMO-SVS and SoulX-Singer-Eval following the\n1695\tSoulX-Singer evaluation protocol. GMO-SVS contains\n1696\t802 samples collected from GTSinger, M4Singer, and\n1697\tOpenCpop. The first sentence of each song is used as\n1698\tthe acoustic prompt, and the remaining sentences are used\n1699\tas synthesis targets. We evaluate the original-lyric score-\n1700\tconditioned setting rather than the rewritten-lyric editing\n1701\tsetting.\n1702\tSoulX-Singer-Eval contains 100 prompt segments from\n1703\t50 unseen singers, including 25 Mandarin and 25 English\n1704\tsingers, with two segments per singer. The Mandarin prompts\n1705\tare collected from professional and amateur singers, and the\n1706\tEnglish prompts are drawn from the Mixing Secrets dataset.\n1707\tTarget lyrics and melodies are selected from 15 Mandarin and\n1708\t15 English tracks in GMO-SVS and paired with manually\n1709\tverified note-level annotations. We use the official same-\n1710\tlanguage Mandarin and English evaluation partitions.\n1711\tMetric Implementation.\n1712\tContent, pitch, and perceptual-\n1713\tquality metrics are computed only on the generated target\n1714\tregion. The acoustic prompt is used only as the reference for\n1715\tspeaker similarity. Mandarin recognition error is reported\n1716\tas CER, although the original SoulX-Singer protocol uses\n1717\tthe term WER for both Mandarin and English recognition\n1718\tresults. On GMO-SVS, an F0 frame is counted as incorrect\n1719\twhen the relative deviation from the reference F0 exceeds\n1720\t20%. Following the official SoulX-Singer-Eval protocol, FFE\n1721\tis not reported on that benchmark. CER, WER, and FFE are\n1722\tmultiplied by 100 and reported as percentages.\n1723\tAdditional Experimental Results\n1724\tThis section distinguishes controlled diagnostic studies from\n1725\tfinal-checkpoint evaluations. Unless otherwise stated, ab-\n1726\tlations of the SA-VAE configuration and state-supervision\n1727\tobjective use the 100K-hour speech-only corpus, a shared\n1728\t0.8B backbone architecture, and a matched budget of 100K\n1729\tupdates. Convergence analyses and final-checkpoint compar-\n1730\tisons follow the full 300K-update schedule and are explicitly\n1731\tidentified. Inference settings are fixed across comparisons,\n1732\texcept when classifier-free guidance (CFG) is varied.\n1733\tSA-VAE Representation Analysis\n1734\tAblations on Alignment Loss and KL Weight.\n1735\tAll con-\n1736\tfigurations use 25-Hz continuous latents and share the same\n1737\tencoder–decoder architecture, training data, optimization\n1738\tbudget, and evaluation protocol. The first two rows isolate\n1739\tthe effect of βKL without semantic alignment, whereas the\n1740\tremaining rows compare alignment losses at βKL = 0.01\n1741\tagainst the matched Vanilla VAE. Reducing the KL weight\n1742\t\n1743\t=== PAGE 12 ===\n1744\tSA-VAE\n1745\tSemBridge Generator\n1746\tSetting\n1747\tValue\n1748\tSetting\n1749\tValue\n1750\tTraining budget\n1751\t300K updates\n1752\tTraining budget\n1753\t300K updates\n1754\tTraining input\n1755\t3s waveforms segments at 44.1 kHz\n1756\tModeling unit\n1757\t12.5-Hz continuous acoustic patches\n1758\tGlobal batch size\n1759\t48 segments\n1760\tGlobal batch size\n1761\t4,096 acoustic-patch frames\n1762\tHardware\n1763\t8× NVIDIA H20\n1764\tHardware\n1765\t16× NVIDIA H20 98 GB\n1766\tPrecision\n1767\tfp32\n1768\tPrecision\n1769\tbfloat16\n1770\tOptimizer\n1771\tAdamW\n1772\tOptimizer\n1773\tAdamW\n1774\tLearning rate\n1775\t1 × 10−4\n1776\tLearning-rate schedule\n1777\tPeak 1 × 10−4; 5K warm-up; cosine decay\n1778\tLoss weights\n1779\tMR-STFT 10; mel 15; KL 0.01; alignment\n1780\t100; adversarial 1.0; feature matching 1.5\n1781\tLoss weights\n1782\tFlow matching 1.0; semantic anchoring 0.1;\n1783\tstop prediction 1.0\n1784\tGlobal seed\n1785\t42\n1786\tGlobal seed\n1787\t42\n1788\tTable 8: Training configurations of SA-VAE and the SemBridge generator.\n1789\tModel\n1790\tAlignment Loss\n1791\tβKL\n1792\tFrame Rate (Hz) PESQ-WB ↑PESQ-NB ↑STOI ↑UTMOS ↑SIM ↑Recon. WER ↓\n1793\tVanilla VAE None\n1794\t5 × 10−5\n1795\t25\n1796\t3.186\n1797\t3.562\n1798\t0.963\n1799\t3.888\n1800\t0.942\n1801\t2.312\n1802\tVanilla VAE None\n1803\t0.01\n1804\t25\n1805\t2.996\n1806\t3.421\n1807\t0.960\n1808\t3.890\n1809\t0.926\n1810\t2.292\n1811\tSA-VAE\n1812\tLMSE\n1813\t0.01\n1814\t25\n1815\t3.071\n1816\t3.505\n1817\t0.959\n1818\t3.914\n1819\t0.939\n1820\t2.317\n1821\tSA-VAE\n1822\tLcos\n1823\t0.01\n1824\t25\n1825\t3.063\n1826\t3.501\n1827\t0.957\n1828\t3.884\n1829\t0.932\n1830\t2.300\n1831\tSA-VAE\n1832\tLcos + L1\n1833\t0.01\n1834\t25\n1835\t2.988\n1836\t3.449\n1837\t0.958\n1838\t3.923\n1839\t0.930\n1840\t2.268\n1841\tTable 9: Reconstruction ablation over KL weights and semantic-alignment losses. Best and second-best results are shown in\n1842\tbold and underlined, respectively.\n1843\tVAE Configuration\n1844\tZH\n1845\tEN\n1846\tZH-Hard\n1847\tCER ↓SIM ↑WER ↓SIM ↑CER ↓SIM ↑\n1848\tVanilla VAE (βKL = 5 × 10−5)\n1849\t1.99\n1850\t0.701\n1851\t3.43\n1852\t0.622\n1853\t17.27 0.664\n1854\tVanilla VAE (βKL = 0.01)\n1855\t1.72\n1856\t0.695\n1857\t3.05\n1858\t0.617\n1859\t15.95 0.654\n1860\tSA-VAE (LMSE)\n1861\t1.48 0.7022\n1862\t3.09\n1863\t0.6293 18.61 0.655\n1864\tSA-VAE (Lcos)\n1865\t1.45\n1866\t0.709\n1867\t2.96\n1868\t0.628\n1869\t16.16 0.663\n1870\tSA-VAE (Lcos + L1; default)\n1871\t1.47\n1872\t0.711\n1873\t2.81\n1874\t0.631\n1875\t15.94 0.664\n1876\tTable 10: Downstream comparison of VAE configura-\n1877\ttions. Best and second-best results are shown in bold and\n1878\tunderlined, respectively.\n1879\tyields the strongest PESQ, STOI, and SIM, but the align-\n1880\tment objectives reveal a trade-off between signal fidelity and\n1881\tsemantic reconstruction. Although LMSE better preserves\n1882\tPESQ and SIM, Lcos + L1 achieves the highest UTMOS\n1883\t(3.923) and lowest reconstruction WER (2.268), improving\n1884\tover the matched baseline at 3.890 and 2.292, respectively.\n1885\tWe therefore adopt Lcos +L1 for its favorable balance of sig-\n1886\tnal fidelity, perceptual quality, and linguistic intelligibility.\n1887\tSA-VAE Provides the Best Downstream Balance.\n1888\tAll\n1889\tvariants use the same 0.8B backbone, 100K-hour speech-\n1890\tonly corpus, 100K-update training budget, GLM-4 semantic-\n1891\ttoken targets, and Anchor@32 configuration, thereby iso-\n1892\tlating the effect of the pretrained and subsequently frozen\n1893\tVAE. Among the Vanilla VAE configurations, increasing\n1894\tβKL from 5×10−5 to 0.01 improves content accuracy on all\n1895\tthree evaluation sets, but consistently reduces SIM. At the\n1896\tmatched βKL = 0.01, the downstream results are sensitive\n1897\tto the alignment objective: LMSE improves ZH CER but de-\n1898\tgrades EN WER and ZH-Hard CER relative to the Vanilla\n1899\tVAE, indicating an inconsistent transfer benefit. In contrast,\n1900\tLcos+L1 obtains the best or tied-best result on five of the six\n1901\tmetrics. Relative to the matched Vanilla VAE, it reduces ZH\n1902\tCER by 14.5% and EN WER by 7.9%, leaves ZH-Hard CER\n1903\teffectively unchanged at 15.94 versus 15.95, and increases\n1904\tSIM by 0.016, 0.014, and 0.010 on ZH, EN, and ZH-Hard, re-\n1905\tspectively. Although Lcos achieves a slightly lower ZH CER\n1906\tthan the default configuration (1.45 versus 1.47), the com-\n1907\tbined objective performs better on each of the remaining\n1908\tfive metrics. Together with its lowest reconstruction WER in\n1909\tTable 9, these results support Lcos + L1 as the default for\n1910\tbalancing content accuracy and speaker similarity.\n1911\tRepresentation-Space Analysis of SA-VAE\n1912\tFigure 2\n1913\tcompares the latent-space organization of the Vanilla VAE\n1914\tand SA-VAE under the same evaluation protocol. The Vanilla\n1915\tVAE representations exhibit substantial overlap across sound\n1916\tcategories, indicating that reconstruction and KL regulariza-\n1917\ttion alone do not explicitly organize the continuous latent\n1918\tspace according to category-relevant content. In contrast,\n1919\tSA-VAE produces visibly more compact and better sepa-\n1920\trated clusters, while preserving a continuous 64-dimensional\n1921\tacoustic representation. This improvement is also reflected\n1922\tby the quantitative measurements: SA-VAE increases the\n1923\tlinear-probe accuracy from 61.50% to 82.00%, correspond-\n1924\ting to an absolute gain of 20.50 percentage points, and im-\n1925\tproves the 5-NN accuracy from 42.25% to 67.00%, an ab-\n1926\tsolute gain of 24.75 percentage points. The higher LP ac-\n1927\tcuracy shows that category-relevant information becomes\n1928\tmore directly accessible through a linear classifier, whereas\n1929\tthe higher 5-NN accuracy indicates that samples from the\n1930\tsame category form more consistent local neighborhoods.\n1931\tSince the category labels are used only for evaluation and\n1932\tare not provided as training targets, these results suggest that\n1933\t\n1934\t=== PAGE 13 ===\n1935\tFigure 2: Representation-space comparison between a\n1936\tVanilla VAE (βKL = 10−2) and the proposed SA-VAE on\n1937\tten ESC-50 sound categories. The t-SNE projections are\n1938\tobtained from temporally pooled 64-dimensional sampled\n1939\tlatents z. Linear-probe (LP) and 5-nearest-neighbor (5-NN)\n1940\taccuracies are computed in the original standardized latent\n1941\tspace rather than in the two-dimensional t-SNE space. Higher\n1942\tvalues indicate stronger linear class separability and more\n1943\tconsistent local neighborhood structure, respectively.\n1944\tTarget\n1945\tLoss λsem\n1946\tZH\n1947\tEN\n1948\tZH-Hard\n1949\tCER ↓SIM ↑WER ↓SIM ↑CER ↓SIM ↑\n1950\tNone\n1951\t–\n1952\t–\n1953\t2.17\n1954\t0.697\n1955\t4.63\n1956\t0.600 20.13 0.656\n1957\tToken Emb. Lcos 0.1\n1958\t2.12\n1959\t0.709\n1960\t4.62\n1961\t0.614 19.43 0.665\n1962\tToken Emb. Lcos 0.5\n1963\t2.01\n1964\t0.709\n1965\t4.13\n1966\t0.618 19.05 0.667\n1967\tToken Emb. Lcos 1.0\n1968\t1.90\n1969\t0.711\n1970\t3.68\n1971\t0.622 18.86 0.669\n1972\tToken ID\n1973\tLCE 0.1\n1974\t1.47\n1975\t0.711\n1976\t2.81\n1977\t0.631 15.94 0.664\n1978\tTable 11: Comparison between continuous token-embedding\n1979\tregression with cosine loss and discrete token-ID classifica-\n1980\ttion with cross-entropy loss. The unanchored model serves\n1981\tas the baseline. Best and second-best results are shown in\n1982\tbold and underlined.\n1983\talignment with the frozen semantic tokenizer regularizes the\n1984\tacoustic latent space toward a more structured representation\n1985\twithout introducing categorical supervision. The t-SNE pro-\n1986\tjections are used only as qualitative illustrations; the quantita-\n1987\ttive conclusion is supported by LP and 5-NN measurements\n1988\tcomputed before dimensionality reduction.\n1989\tAnalysis of Semantic-Token Anchoring\n1990\tEmbedding Regression versus Token Classification.\n1991\tTa-\n1992\tble 11 compares continuous token-embedding regression\n1993\tbased on cosine loss with discrete token-ID classification\n1994\tbased on cross-entropy loss. Both objectives use targets from\n1995\tthe same frozen semantic tokenizer and are applied to the\n1996\tsame Transformer block. All other model, data, optimiza-\n1997\ttion, and inference settings are identical.\n1998\tFor continuous embedding regression, increasing λsem\n1999\tfrom 0.1 to 1.0 consistently reduces all three content-error\n2000\tmetrics without decreasing SIM. Therefore, λsem = 1.0 is\n2001\tthe best continuous-target configuration among those eval-\n2002\tuated. Nevertheless, discrete token-ID classification with\n2003\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa", "uuid": "60dc56d5-f607-429c-b0d0-a1935c5fe9eb", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T07:06:47.602Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/779e1967-e735-49f9-b3db-e546588afbfa/tool-results/b3fazv1uw.txt", "content": "Looking in indexes: http://nexus.sii.shaipower.online/repository/pypi/simple\nRequirement already satisfied: pymupdf in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (1.27.2.3)\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nSemBridge: Semantic Token Anchoring for Continuous-Latent\nAutoregressive Speech Generation\nHanke Xie1,2∗†, Haopeng Lin2†, Jiale Qian2, Dake Guo1, Yuepeng Jiang1, Zhichao Wang2, Wenxiao\nCao2, Jingbin Hu1, Guobin Ma1, Wenhao Li1, Huakang Chen1, Chengyou Wang1, Ming Tao2,\nZhonghua Fu1, Lei Xie1‡, Xinsheng Wang2‡\n1Audio, Speech and Language Processing Group (ASLP@NPU), School of Software\nNorthwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, China\n2Soul AI Lab, China\nhkxie@mail.nwpu.edu.cn, lxie@nwpu.edu.cn, wangxinsheng@soulapp.cn\nAbstract\nContinuous-latent\nautoregressive\nspeech\ngeneration\nhas\nemerged as a promising alternative to discrete-token modeling\nby avoiding quantization loss and preserving richer acoustic\ninformation. However, continuous acoustic targets do not ex-\npose linguistic structure as explicit token-level prediction tar-\ngets. Consequently, the autoregressive language model (LM)\nmust acquire linguistic structure indirectly through acous-\ntic prediction, which can compromise the content fidelity of\ngenerated speech. We propose SemBridge, a training-only\nsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\nautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge uses discrete se-\nmantic tokens to directly supervise autoregressive LM states\nand employs a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE to organize\nthe continuous target space under the same semantic refer-\nence. The semantic supervision is used only during train-\ning, while inference remains entirely continuous. We evalu-\nate SemBridge on zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) and score-\nconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS). Across multi-\nple benchmarks, SemBridge improves content accuracy, as\nmeasured by word and character error rates (WER/CER),\nwhile maintaining competitive speaker similarity and percep-\ntual quality. Experimental results demonstrate that explicit\nsemantic-token supervision for autoregressive state learning\nis an effective and general direction for continuous speech\ngeneration. Speech samples are available.1 The model code\nand checkpoints will be available at https://github.com/ASLP-\nlab/SemBridge.\nIntroduction\nAutoregressive (AR) models have emerged as a dominant\nparadigm for speech synthesis. (Wang et al. 2023; Anas-\ntassiou et al. 2024). Most AR speech generation systems\nadopt a language-modeling formulation, representing speech\nas discrete token sequences and formulating generation as\nstandard next-token prediction. Recent AR systems fur-\nther advance discrete speech representations by introducing\n1https://tiamojames.github.io/SemBridge_Demo/\n∗Work done during an internship at Soul AI Lab.\n†These authors contributed equally.\n‡Corresponding author.\nlow-rate and semantically structured speech tokens through\npretrained speech representations or hierarchical semantic–\nacoustic modeling (Du et al. 2024b, 2025; Hu et al. 2026;\nZhang et al. 2024a). These designs provide compact and lin-\nguistically meaningful prediction targets, reducing the text–\nspeech semantic gap and improving autoregressive model-\ning. However, discrete representations inevitably introduce\nquantization constraints, limiting their capacity to preserve\nfine-grained acoustic details required for high-fidelity speech\ngeneration. Continuous representations avoid quantization\nloss while providing a richer and more expressive acoustic\ntarget space. Consequently, continuous-latent autoregressive\ngeneration has emerged as a promising direction for high-\nfidelity speech synthesis (Meng et al. 2025; Jia et al. 2025;\nXia et al. 2026).\nContinuous-latent autoregressive models generate speech\nby predicting continuous acoustic representations, such as\nmel-spectrograms or learned acoustic latents, often using\ndiffusion- or flow-based next-patch generation to model com-\nplex acoustic distributions\n(Jia et al. 2025; Peng et al.\n2026; Zhou et al. 2025b). These continuous targets preserve\nrich acoustic information while implicitly encoding linguis-\ntic content. Unlike discrete semantic tokens, however, they\ndo not provide explicit semantic targets at each generation\nstep. Consequently, the autoregressive LM is required to infer\nlinguistic structure indirectly from the continuous generation\nobjective, without explicit token-level semantic supervision.\nRecent studies have explored various strategies for in-\ncorporating semantic information into continuous speech\ngeneration. Existing approaches mainly leverage continuous\nsemantic representations extracted from pretrained speech\nmodels to enhance either target representations or autore-\ngressive hidden states (Wang et al. 2026; An et al. 2026).\nWhile these methods demonstrate the importance of seman-\ntic modeling for continuous generation, they formulate se-\nmantic supervision as continuous feature alignment. In con-\ntrast, discrete semantic tokens organize linguistic content into\ndiscrete semantic units and provide explicit prediction tar-\ngets for autoregressive language modeling. Such discretiza-\ntion makes clusterable linguistic structure explicit while sup-\npressing linguistically irrelevant variation retained in con-\narXiv:2608.07462v1  [eess.AS]  7 Aug 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\ntinuous speech features (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen, Sagot,\nand Dupoux 2022). However, leveraging discrete semantic\ntokens to explicitly supervise continuous-latent autoregres-\nsive states remains underexplored. Other approaches intro-\nduce hierarchical or semi-discrete architectures to combine\nsemantic and acoustic modeling (Zhou et al. 2025b; Yu et al.\n2025); however, they modify the original continuous genera-\ntion interface by introducing additional semantic generation\ncomponents.\nTo address this gap, we introduce SemBridge, a training-\nonly semantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-\nlatent autoregressive speech generation. At its core, Sem-\nBridge uses discrete semantic-token labels to provide direct\nsemantic supervision for autoregressive LM states, while\ncontinuous latent patches remain the sole generated and\nrecurrent variables. A complementary Semantic-Aligned\nAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE) further aligns continuous acoustic\nlatents with token-level embeddings from the same semantic\ntokenizer, establishing a shared semantic reference for latent\nrepresentation learning and state supervision.\nWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot text-to-\nspeech (TTS) and further assess its transferability to score-\nconditioned singing voice synthesis (SVS) (Liu et al. 2022;\nZhang et al. 2022; Qian et al. 2026). Across multiple\nTTS and SVS benchmarks, SemBridge consistently reduces\nWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker similar-\nity and perceptual quality. Controlled ablations show that\nsemantic-token anchoring provides complementary gains be-\nyond target-space alignment alone, while layer-wise analyses\nreveal a trade-off between content accuracy and synthesis\nquality.\nOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n• We propose SemBridge, a training-only semantic-token\nanchoring framework that directly supervises autoregres-\nsive LM states with discrete semantic-token labels while\npreserving continuous-only generation during inference.\n• We introduce a unified semantic supervision scheme\nbased on a shared frozen semantic tokenizer: token-level\nembeddings align continuous acoustic latents through\nSA-VAE, while discrete semantic-token labels explicitly\nsupervise autoregressive LM hidden states.\n• Experiments on zero-shot TTS and score-conditioned\nSVS\nshow\nthat\nSemBridge\nconsistently\nimproves\nWER/CER while maintaining competitive speaker sim-\nilarity and perceptual quality. Ablations further confirm\nthe complementary benefits of latent alignment and state\nanchoring and reveal a trade-off effect of anchoring depth.\nRelated Work\nDiscrete-Token\nAutoregressive\nSpeech\nGeneration.\nModern LM-based speech systems commonly represent\nspeech as discrete token sequences produced by semantic\nor neural-codec tokenizers. AudioLM, VALL-E, and Seed-\nTTS formulate speech generation as autoregressive sequence\nmodeling over discrete speech representations, enabling scal-\nable generation and zero-shot adaptation (Borsos et al. 2022;\nWang et al. 2023; Anastassiou et al. 2024). Recent work\nincreasingly explores low-rate and semantically informed to-\nkens to shorten speech sequences and strengthen linguistic\nmodeling. SpeechTokenizer distributes semantic and acous-\ntic information across multiple quantization levels, while\nWavTokenizer explores a compact single-codebook repre-\nsentation (Zhang et al. 2024a; Ji et al. 2025). Self-supervised\nspeech studies further show that learned representations ex-\nhibit clusterable phonetic structure, while discrete-unit analy-\nses demonstrate that discretization can suppress linguistically\nirrelevant variation and provide effective prediction targets\nfor spoken language modeling (Hsu et al. 2021; Nguyen,\nSagot, and Dupoux 2022). Nevertheless, quantization intro-\nduces an information bottleneck that can limit the preserva-\ntion of fine-grained acoustic details.\nContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Speech Generation.\nContinuous-latent approaches avoid discrete quantization by\ndirectly modeling mel-spectrograms, learned acoustic la-\ntents, or latent distributions. MELLE autoregressively gen-\nerates continuous mel-spectrogram frames, while KALL-E\nmodels the distribution of future continuous latents (Meng\net al. 2025; Xia et al. 2026). Recent methods further combine\ncausal sequence modeling with diffusion- or flow-based local\ngeneration. DiTAR separates inter-patch dependency model-\ning from intra-patch acoustic generation through a causal\nLM and a local diffusion transformer (Jia et al. 2025), while\nVibeVoiceuseslow-rate continuousrepresentationsandnext-\npatch diffusion for long-form multi-speaker generation (Peng\net al. 2026). These approaches preserve an expressive contin-\nuous acoustic target space, but their autoregressive states are\ntrained primarily through continuous generation objectives.\nSemantic Guidance for Continuous Autoregressive Gen-\neration.\nRecent methods introduce semantic guidance into\ncontinuous generation through pretrained speech representa-\ntions. SemaVoice aligns continuous VAE latents with high-\nlevel features from a frozen speech foundation model, im-\nproving semantic structure in the acoustic target space be-\nfore autoregressive training (Wang et al. 2026). MELA-TTS\ninstead regularizes autoregressive states by aligning them\nwith continuous ASR features (An et al. 2026). These meth-\nods transfer semantic information through continuous feature\nalignment at the target and predictor levels, respectively. Vox-\nCPM adopts a semi-discrete semantic-prosodic bottleneck\ntogether with a residual pathway, retaining an explicit seman-\ntic modeling branch during inference (Zhou et al. 2025b).\nMethod\nOverview\nSemBridge is built on a continuous-latent autoregressive\nbackbone that generates acoustic latent patches conditioned\non symbolic inputs and preceding acoustic history. Its core\nmechanism is semantic-token anchoring, which uses dis-\ncrete semantic-token labels to provide direct supervision\nfor selected autoregressive LM states. A complementary\nSemantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) organizes the\ncontinuous acoustic target space under the same semantic\nreference. Together, the two objectives introduce semantic\nstructure into both the predictive LM states and the contin-\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nSemantic   Anchoring\nSemantic\nEncoder\nAcoustic\nEncoder\nVariational\nPosterior\nVQ\nSemantic Token\nAlign Loss\nAcoustic \nReconstruction\nLoss\nAcoustic\nDecoder\nSemantic\nProjector\nDecoder-Only Transformer\nUnified Prefix\nTask\nText\nMiDi\nPatch Enc\nPatch Enc\n(A) Semantic-Aligned Acoustic Latent Tokenizer\n(B) SemBridge: Next Acoustic-Latent Prediction \nLocDiT\nLocDiT\nLocDiT\nSemantic Feature\nAcoustic Feature\nSemantic Token\nAcoustic Latent\nPatch Enc\nPatch Latent \nText Sequence\nText Embedding\nTraining only\nTraining & Inference\nCe Loss \n \nSemantic Head\nHidden State \nInference only\nFigure 1: Overview of the two-stage SemBridge framework. (A) Stage I trains SA-VAE to reconstruct waveforms while aligning\ncontinuous acoustic patches with embeddings from a frozen semantic tokenizer; their token IDs are retained as Stage-II targets.\n(B) Stage II trains a continuous-latent autoregressive generator: a causal LM encodes symbolic conditions and preceding acoustic\npatches, LocDiT predicts the next patch, and a semantic head supervises a LM hidden layer under the same causal shift.\nuous targets they model, while retaining continuous latent\npatches as the sole generated and recurrent variables.\nWe use an open-source pretrained GLM-4 tokenizer(Zeng\net al. 2024) operating at 12.5 Hz. Given a training waveform\nx, the tokenizer produces a sequence of token-level semantic\nembeddings and their corresponding discrete token IDs:\n(E, S) = SGLM(x), E = {et}T\nt=1, S = {st}T\nt=1,\n(1)\nwhere et denotes the continuous embedding associated with\nthe semantic token st, and st ∈{1, . . . , V } with vocabu-\nlary size V = 16,384. The embeddings and token IDs pro-\nvide two complementary forms of supervision: et is used for\ncontinuous target-space alignment, whereas st serves as the\ncategorical target for LM-state anchoring.\nThe SA-VAE encoder produces frame-level continuous\nacoustic latents A = {an}2T\nn=1 at 25 Hz. We group every two\nconsecutive frames into one autoregressive acoustic patch:\nzt = Concat (a2t−1, a2t) ,\nt = 1, . . . , T.\n(2)\nThe resulting acoustic patch sequence Z = {zt}T\nt=1 therefore\nhas the same 12.5-Hz rate as the semantic tokenizer outputs.\nThis establishes a one-to-one temporal correspondence:\nzt ←→(et, st) .\n(3)\nAccordingly, each continuous patch is aligned with a token-\nlevel embedding during SA-VAE training, and the LM state\nresponsible for predicting that patch is supervised by the\ncorresponding discrete token ID during generator training.\nTraining proceeds in two stages as shown in Figure 1.\nStage I trains SA-VAE with acoustic reconstruction and se-\nmantic target-alignment objectives. Stage II freezes SA-VAE\nand the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer, and trains the continuous\nautoregressive generator with its original generation objec-\ntives together with semantic-token anchoring. Although SA-\nVAE is trained first, the defining component of SemBridge\nis the Stage-II token-level supervision applied directly to au-\ntoregressive LM states.\nContinuous-Latent Autoregressive Backbone\nGiven the continuous acoustic patch sequence Z\n=\n{z1, . . . , zT } produced by the frozen SA-VAE, SemBridge\nautoregressively models speech generation conditioned on a\nsymbolic input c and an optional acoustic prompt r:\np(Z | c, r) =\nT\nY\nt=1\np (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n(4)\nAutoregressive\nContext\nModeling.\nFollowing\nrecent\ncontinuous-latent autoregressive frameworks\n(Jia et al.\n2025), the generator consists of PatchEnc, a causal LM, and\na local diffusion transformer, denoted as LocDiT. PatchEnc\nmaps the acoustic prompt and preceding continuous patches\ninto acoustic context representations, which are combined\nwith the symbolic conditions and processed by the causal\nLM. At the position responsible for predicting zt, the hidden\nstate produced by Transformer layer ℓis:\nh(ℓ)\nt\n= H(ℓ)\nθ\n(c, r, z<t) ,\nℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n(5)\nThe causal construction ensures that h(ℓ)\nt\nonly depends on the\nsymbolic conditions, prompt context, and preceding acoustic\npatches. The final-layer state provides the global condition\nfor continuous next-patch generation, while a selected hid-\nden layer is additionally supervised by the semantic-token\nanchoring objective described in Section Semantic-Token\nAnchoring.\nConditional Continuous Patch Generation.\nThe normal-\nized final LM state\ngt = RMSNorm\n\u0010\nh(L)\nt\n\u0011\n(6)\nconditions LocDiT together with the preceding acoustic\npatch zt−1. Rather than directly regressing zt, LocDiT mod-\nels its conditional distribution using flow matching:\nLFM = E\nh\n∥vΘ (ezt,τ, τ; gt, zt−1) −v⋆\nt ∥2\n2\ni\n,\n(7)\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nwhere τ denotes the flow time, ezt,τ is the corresponding\nintermediate flow state, and v⋆\nt is the target velocity field.\nA lightweight stop predictor attached to the final LM state\ndetermines sequence termination.\nSemantic-Token Anchoring\nCausally Aligned Semantic Prediction.\nLet h(ℓ)\nt\ndenote\nthe post-block hidden state produced by Transformer layer ℓ\nat the autoregressive position responsible for predicting the\nnext continuous patch zt:\nh(ℓ)\nt\n= H(ℓ)\nθ\n(c, r, z<t) ,\nℓ= 1, . . . , L.\n(8)\nBecause the GLM-4 semantic tokenizer and the autoregres-\nsive acoustic patches share the same 12.5-Hz temporal rate,\nthe semantic token st is associated with the same target patch\nzt. Under the causal shift used for next-patch prediction, the\nstate h(ℓ)\nt\nhas access only to the symbolic conditions, prompt\ncontext, and preceding acoustic patches z<t. It does not ob-\nserve either the target patch zt or its semantic label st.\nThe same autoregressive state is therefore trained to sup-\nport two complementary predictions:\nh(L)\nt\n−→zt,\nh(ℓ⋆)\nt\n−→st,\n(9)\nwhere the final-layer state conditions continuous next-patch\ngeneration, while a selected intermediate or final layer ℓ⋆\nis anchored by the corresponding semantic-token label. Se-\nmantic tokens are used only as supervision targets and never\nenter the autoregressive context.\nDepth-Selective\nToken\nClassification.\nWe\nattach\na\nlightweight semantic prediction head fϕ to the post-block\nhidden state at a selected Transformer depth ℓ⋆. The condi-\ntional semantic-token distribution is:\nqϕ\n\u0010\nst | h(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\n= softmax\n\u0010\nfϕ\n\u0010\nh(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\u0011\n.\n(10)\nUnlike continuous feature alignment, this objective does not\nrequire the autoregressive state to reproduce the full geome-\ntry of a teacher representation. Instead, it assigns an explicit\nsemantic classification target to each state responsible for\ncontinuous next-patch prediction.\nThe anchoring objective is computed over all valid acous-\ntic prediction positions:\nLsem = −1\n|T |\nX\nt∈T\nlog qϕ\n\u0010\nst | h(ℓ⋆)\nt\n\u0011\n,\n(11)\nwhere T denotes the set of valid autoregressive acoustic\npositions after excluding padded positions. We allow the at-\ntachment depth ℓ⋆to vary and study its effect experimentally.\nJoint\nContinuous\nand\nSemantic\nObjectives.\nThe\nsemantic-token objective is jointly optimized with the origi-\nnal continuous-latent generation losses:\nLgen = LFM + λsemLsem + λstopLstop.\n(12)\nThe flow-matching objective trains continuous next-patch\ngeneration, while Lsem updates the semantic head and the\nautoregressive LM states. The SA-VAE and GLM-4 semantic\ntokenizer remain frozen throughout Stage II. Consequently,\nthe semantic objective acts as a state-level training constraint\nwithout changing the acoustic target or introducing discrete\nvariables into the recurrent generation history.\nContinuous-Only\nInference.\nAt\ninference,\nneither\nsemantic-token labels nor the semantic prediction head\nare required. Generation follows the original continuous\nPatchEnc–LM–LocDiT pathway:\np(Z | c, r) =\nT\nY\nt=1\np (zt | c, r, z<t) .\n(13)\nContinuous latent patches remain the only generated vari-\nables and recurrent acoustic history. The semantic-token\nbranch therefore changes how the autoregressive states are\nlearned, rather than how speech is generated.\nTarget-Space Alignment with SA-VAE\nComplementary to state-level semantic-token anchoring, we\nintroduce a Semantic-Aligned Acoustic VAE (SA-VAE) to\nimprove the semantic correspondence of the continuous\nacoustic target space. SA-VAE remains a continuous Gaus-\nsian VAE and uses a frozen semantic tokenizer as its se-\nmantic reference. Given an input waveform x, the tokenizer\nextracts token-level semantic embeddings e = Sfeat(x). A\nlightweight projection maps the continuous acoustic latent\nrepresentations into the semantic feature space, and seman-\ntic correspondence is optimized through:\nLalign = Dsem (Pz(z), sg [e]) ,\n(14)\nwhere Pzdenote lightweight alignment mappings, sg(·) stops\ngradients through the frozen semantic tokenizer, and Dsem\ncombines cosine alignment with a weak L1 constraint.\nThe complete Stage-I objective is:\nLSA-VAE = LVAE + λalignLalign.\n(15)\nThis objective encourages patch-level semantic correspon-\ndence without imposing a discrete bottleneck on the acoustic\nrepresentation. After Stage I training, SA-VAE is frozen and\nprovides continuous acoustic targets for generator training.\nTarget-space alignment and state-level anchoring use dif-\nferent forms of supervision from the same semantic tok-\nenizer. SA-VAE aligns zt with the continuous embedding\net, whereas semantic-token anchoring uses the correspond-\ning discrete ID st to supervise the LM state responsible for\npredicting zt. Their shared semantic identity and temporal\ncorrespondence make the two objectives complementary.\nTask Conditioning for TTS and SVS\nSemantic-token anchoring is applied to the autoregressive\nLM states responsible for continuous next-patch prediction\nand is shared across TTS and SVS. TTS uses text and lan-\nguage conditions, whereas score-conditioned SVS addition-\nally incorporates lyrics and score-derived pitch and duration;\nboth tasks otherwise share the same continuous acoustic rep-\nresentation, PatchEnc–LM–LocDiT backbone, and anchor-\ning objective. SVS therefore provides a more demanding\nextension for examining whether semantic-token supervi-\nsion improves lyric intelligibility under explicit musical con-\nstraints without sacrificing the fine-grained acoustic variation\nand expressiveness of singing.\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nModel\nParams Data (h)\nContent Consistency ↓| Speaker Similarity ↑\nSeed-TTS-Eval\nCV3-EVAL\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nEN-Hard\nReported and open-source systems\nF5-TTS\n0.3B\n100k\n1.53 | 0.760\n2.00 | 0.670\n8.67 | 0.713\n5.47 | –\n8.90 | –\n– | –\n– | –\nCosyVoice2\n0.5B\n170k\n1.38 | 0.757\n3.09 | 0.659\n6.83 | 0.724\n4.08 | –\n6.32 | –\n12.58 | 0.726\n11.96 | 0.667\nCosyVoice3-1.5B\n1.5B\n1.0M\n1.12 | 0.781\n2.22 | 0.720\n5.83 | 0.758\n3.91 | –\n4.99 | –\n9.77 | 0.785\n10.55 | 0.761\nIndexTTS2\n1.5B\n–\n1.03 | 0.765\n2.23 | 0.706\n7.12 | 0.755\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nHiggsAudio-v2\n3B\n10M\n1.50 | 0.740\n2.44 | 0.677\n55.07 | 0.656\n3.89 | –\n5.24 | –\n14.14 | 0.786\n9.04 | 0.759\nContinuous-latent autoregressive systems\nDiTAR\n0.6B\n–\n1.02 | 0.753\n1.69 | 0.735\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nMELA-TTS\n0.8B\n170k\n0.95 | 0.720\n2.45 | 0.590\n7.75 | 0.710\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nVoxCPM-Emilia\n0.6B\n100k\n1.11 | 0.740\n2.34 | 0.681\n12.46 | 0.698\n4.47 | –\n5.23 | –\n22.20 | 0.626\n10.00 | 0.626\nVoxCPM\n0.6B\n1.8M\n0.93 | 0.772\n1.85 | 0.729\n8.87 | 0.730\n3.40 | –\n4.04 | –\n12.90 | 0.661\n7.89 | 0.643\nSemaVoice\n1.5B\n100k\n1.32 | 0.728\n1.91 | 0.657\n9.37 | 0.687\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\n– | –\nVibeVoice∗\n1.5B\n–\n2.78 | 0.686\n5.28 | 0.588\n29.43 | 0.583\n5.16 | 0.702\n6.96 | 0.560\n15.94 | 0.637\n7.89 | 0.586\nVoxCPM2\n2B\n2M\n0.97 | 0.795\n1.84 | 0.753\n8.13 | 0.753\n3.65 | –\n5.00 | –\n8.55 | –\n8.48 | –\nSemBridge\n0.8B\n120k\n0.95 | 0.758\n1.81 | 0.699\n9.79 | 0.717\n3.34 | 0.757\n4.22 | 0.658\n10.58 | 0.717\n6.35 | 0.619\nTable 1: Objective evaluation on Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL. Each result is reported as content consistency error | speaker\nsimilarity, where Chinese subsets use CER, English subsets use WER, and speaker similarity is measured by SIM. Lower\ncontent error and higher SIM indicate better performance. “–” denotes unavailable results. Bold and underlined values indicate\nthe best and second-best results, respectively. Tied second-best results are both underlined. ∗denotes results obtained through\nour evaluation.\nExperiments\nExperimental Setup\nDatasets.\nWe evaluate SemBridge primarily on zero-shot\ntext-to-speech (TTS), with score-conditioned singing voice\nsynthesis (SVS) as a cross-task extension. SA-VAE is trained\nseparately on 25K hours of audio, including 20K hours of\nspeech sampled from public corpora and a 5K-hour subset\nof our internal singing data. All matched TTS models and\ncomponent ablations are trained on the 100K-hour bilingual\nopen-source VoxBox corpus (Wang et al. 2025). For joint\nTTS and SVS training, we augment VoxBox with the full\n20K hours of internal singing data, resulting in a 120K-hour\nmultitask corpus. We use the 100K-hour models for con-\ntrolled component attribution and the 120K-hour model for\nsystem-level comparison and SVS evaluation. Further details\non corpus composition and preprocessing are provided in the\nsupplementary material.\nModel Architecture.\nSemBridge consists of a continuous-\nlatent autoregressive backbone, a frozen Semantic-Aligned\nAcoustic VAE (SA-VAE), and training-time semantic super-\nvision modules. The causal LM contains 32 Transformer\nblocks with a hidden size of 1,024 and 16 attention heads.\nPatchEnc and LocDiT each contain 8 blocks with the same\nhidden size and number of heads. SA-VAE is a causal con-\nvolutional Gaussian VAE that encodes 44.1 kHz waveforms\ninto 64-dimensional continuous latent frames at 25 Hz with-\nout vector quantization. The frozen semantic tokenizer op-\nerates at 12.5 Hz with a vocabulary of 16,384 tokens. Dur-\ning Stage I, a lightweight projection aligns SA-VAE acous-\ntic patches with token-level semantic embeddings. During\nStage II, the corresponding discrete token labels supervise\nautoregressive LM states. We denote a semantic head at-\ntached after Transformer block k as Anchor@k.\nExcluding the frozen SA-VAE, the continuous generation\nbackbone contains 800.15M trainable parameters. SA-VAE\ncontains 86.48M parameters, while the alignment projec-\ntion and semantic anchoring module add 2.43M and 16.79M\nparameters, respectively. The alignment projection and se-\nmantic head are used only during training and are removed\nat inference.\nTraining Details.\nWe train SA-VAE independently for\n300K updates on 3.0-second audio segments with a batch\nsize of 48 using 8 NVIDIA H20 GPUs. We then train Sem-\nBridge for 300K updates using 16 NVIDIA H20 GPUs and\na global batch size of 4,096 acoustic-patch frames. We use\nAdamW (Loshchilov and Hutter 2019) with bfloat16 pre-\ncision. The learning rate is linearly increased to 1 × 10−4\nover the first 5K updates and then decayed with a cosine\nschedule. The flow-matching and stop-prediction losses are\nassigned unit weights, and the semantic anchoring weight is\nset to λsem = 0.1. Inference uses 10 function evaluations\n(NFE = 10), a classifier-free guidance scale of 2.0 (Ho and\nSalimans 2022), and a sampling temperature of 1.0.\nEvaluation Metrics.\nWe evaluate zero-shot TTS on Seed-\nTTS-Eval (Anastassiou et al. 2024) and CV3-Eval (Du et al.\n2025). Content fidelity is measured by character error rate\n(CER) for Chinese using Paraformer (Gao et al. 2022) and\nword error rate (WER) for English using Whisper-large-v3\n(Radford et al. 2023). Speaker similarity (SIM) is computed\nas the cosine similarity between WavLM-based speaker em-\nbeddings\n(Chen et al. 2022), and UTMOS (Saeki et al.\n2022) is used as an automatic estimate of perceptual qual-\nity. We evaluate SA-VAE reconstruction using PESQ (Rix\net al. 2001), STOI (Taal et al. 2011), and UTMOS. For SVS,\nwe follow the GMO-SVS evaluation protocol (Qian et al.\n2026) and report CER/WER, SIM, F0 frame error (FFE),\nSingMOS-Pro (Tang et al. 2025), and Sheet scores.\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nModel\nZH\nEN\nZH-Hard\nCER ↓SIM ↑WER ↓SIM ↑CER ↓SIM ↑UTMOS ↑\nContinuous-AR Baselines\nVoxCPM\n1.52\n0.743\n2.46\n0.675 14.29 0.704\n2.651\n+SA-VAE\n1.63\n0.746\n2.22\n0.683 13.72 0.704\n2.647\nSemBridge Alignment–Anchoring\n-Align,-Anchor\n1.58\n0.745\n2.43\n0.685 16.87 0.696\n2.622\n+Align\n1.51\n0.735\n2.30\n0.678 15.94 0.697\n2.620\n+Anchor\n1.21\n0.742\n2.18\n0.683 13.97 0.700\n2.659\n+Align,+Anchor 1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nAnchoring Depth λsem = 0.1\nNo Anchor\n1.51\n0.735\n2.30\n0.678 15.94 0.697\n2.620\nAnchor@18\n1.13\n0.747\n2.12\n0.688 12.68 0.699\n2.656\nAnchor@24\n1.04\n0.747\n1.93\n0.693 12.01 0.704\n2.683\nAnchor@32\n1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nAnchoring Strength Anchor@32\nλsem = 1.0\n1.64\n0.681\n5.11\n0.591 24.31 0.632\n2.588\nλsem = 0.5\n1.61\n0.705\n3.64\n0.623 16.25 0.663\n2.614\nλsem = 0.1\n1.01\n0.743\n1.87\n0.687 11.87 0.697\n2.648\nλsem = 0.05\n1.13\n0.746\n2.32\n0.683 13.86 0.700\n2.652\nTable 2: Ablation study of target-space semantic alignment\nand predictor-state semantic anchoring. All variants use the\nsame 0.8B backbone, 100K hours of training data, and 300K\nupdates. The default SemBridge configuration uses SA-VAE\nwith semantic alignment and Anchor@32. UTMOS is re-\nported on ZH-Hard for perceptual quality. Bold and under-\nlined values indicate the globally best and second-best re-\nsults, respectively. Tied results receive the same formatting.\nBaselines.\nFor zero-shot TTS, we compare SemBridge\nwith representative systems from several speech genera-\ntion paradigms. Non-autoregressive baselines include F5-\nTTS (Chen et al. 2024) and MaskGCT (Wang et al. 2024),\nwhich use flow-matching and masked-generation formula-\ntions, respectively. Discrete-token autoregressive systems in-\nclude the CosyVoice series (Du et al. 2024a,b, 2025), Spark-\nTTS\n(Wang et al. 2025), FireRedTTS (Guo et al. 2024),\nHiggsAudio-v2(Boson AI 2026),and IndexTTS2 (Zhou et al.\n2025a). We also compare with continuous-latent or hybrid\nautoregressive systems, including DiTAR (Jia et al. 2025),\nVoxCPM (Zhou et al. 2025b), VoxCPM2 (Zhou et al. 2026),\nVibeVoice (Peng et al. 2026), MELA-TTS (An et al. 2026),\nand SemaVoice (Wang et al. 2026).\nFor score-conditioned svs, we compare with representative\nrecent systems on GMO-SVS, including StyleSinger (Zhang\net al. 2024b), TCSinger (Zhang et al. 2024c), Vevo (Zhang\net al. 2025), Ying-Music-Singer (Zheng et al. 2025), and\nSoulX-Singer (Qian et al. 2026). Baseline results are taken\nfrom the original publications or obtained using official\ncheckpoints when available. The supplementary material re-\nports the model scale, training data, evaluation source, and\nimplementation details for each comparison.\nExperimental Results\nMain Results on Zero-Shot TTS.\nTable 1 reports zero-\nshot TTS results on Seed-TTS-Eval and CV3-EVAL. The\nsystem-level SemBridge model, trained on the 120K-hour\nmultitask corpus, achieves consistently low content error\nacross languages and evaluation conditions while maintain-\nModel\nRate PESQ STOI UTMOS\nReference Audio\n–\n4.64\n1.00\n4.09\nDiscrete tokenizers\nEncodec\n600\n2.77\n0.94\n3.09\nEncodec\n150\n1.56\n0.85\n1.58\nDAC\n400\n4.01\n0.95\n4.00\nSAC\n37.5\n2.21\n0.90\n4.28\nMimi\n100\n2.26\n0.91\n3.63\nMimi\n200\n2.88\n0.94\n3.87\nWavTokenizer\n40\n1.64\n0.85\n3.03\nX-codec2\n50\n2.43\n0.92\n4.13\nBiCodec\n50\n2.51\n0.92\n4.18\nContinuous tokenizer\nVoxCPM-VAE\n25\n3.23\n0.96\n3.96\nVibeVoice-VAE\n7.5\n2.98\n0.75\n4.18\nSA-VAE\n25\n2.99\n0.96\n3.92\nw/o Alignment\n25\n3.00\n0.96\n3.92\nTable 3: Reconstruction quality of representative discrete\ntokenizers and continuous tokenizer. Rate is measured in\nframes or tokens per second; higher is better for all metrics.\nBold and underlined values indicate the globally best and\nsecond-best results among tokenizers, respectively.\ning competitive speaker similarity. On Seed-TTS-Eval, it ob-\ntains a CER of 0.95 on Chinese, a WER of 1.81 on English,\nand a CER of 9.79 on the Chinese hard subset. On CV3-\nEVAL, it achieves a CER of 3.34 on Chinese, a WER of 4.22\non English, a CER of 10.58 on the Chinese hard subset, and\na WER of 6.35 on the English hard subset. Compared with\nMELA-TTS, which achieves competitive content accuracy\nbut lower speaker similarity, particularly on English, Sem-\nBridge provides a more balanced content–speaker trade-off.\nThis pattern is consistent with token-level classification pro-\nviding a more focused semantic regularizer than full contin-\nuous feature matching, though the cross-system comparison\ndoes not establish causality.\nControlled Analysis of Semantic-Token Anchoring\nAcoustic Representation Control.\nTable 2 reports con-\ntrolled experiments using the same 0.8B model scale, 100K\nhours of training data, and 300K updates. For the continuous-\nAR baseline, replacing the original acoustic representation\nwith SA-VAE reduces English WER from 2.46 to 2.22 and\nZH-Hard CER from 14.29 to 13.72, while Chinese CER\nchanges from 1.52 to 1.63. Although gains vary across sub-\nsets, the results show that acoustic representation affects\ncontent modeling and motivate the subsequent component\nanalysis under a fixed SemBridge backbone.\nAlignment and Anchoring Attribution.\nWithin the Sem-\nBridge backbone, the model without target alignment or state\nanchoring obtains errors of 1.58, 2.43, and 16.87 on ZH, EN,\nand ZH-Hard, respectively. Target-space alignment alone re-\nduces these errors to 1.51, 2.30, and 15.94, whereas semantic-\ntoken anchoring produces larger reductions to 1.21, 2.18, and\n13.97. Combining the two objectives gives the lowest errors\nof 1.01, 1.87, and 11.87. These results indicate that state-\nlevel semantic-token anchoring drives most content-fidelity\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\nMandarin\nEnglish\nSystem\nCER (%) ↓SIM ↑FFE (%) ↓SingMOS ↑Sheet ↑WER (%) ↓SIM ↑FFE (%) ↓SingMOS ↑Sheet ↑\nReference and reconstruction bounds\nReference Audio\n7.40\n–\n–\n4.624\n4.334\n19.70\n–\n–\n4.441\n3.825\nExisting SVS systems\nStyleSinger\n36.70\n0.817\n36.30\n3.938\n3.819\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\nTCSinger\n27.00\n0.855\n31.50\n3.977\n3.843\n41.00\n0.879\n21.10\n3.662\n3.482\nVevo\n30.80\n0.897\n11.20\n4.355\n4.005\n48.40\n0.924\n8.80\n4.321\n3.699\nYing-Music-Singer\n9.90\n0.902\n13.20\n4.145\n3.620\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\nSoulX-Singer\n6.90\n0.905\n12.20\n4.445\n4.107\n14.90\n0.926\n16.40\n4.303\n3.705\nContinuous-latent autoregressive system\nSemBridge(ours)\n8.32\n0.906\n16.70\n4.585\n4.315\n14.77\n0.926\n25.89\n4.444\n3.994\nw/o Anchoring\n9.18\n0.905\n16.74\n4.560\n4.306\n16.29\n0.926\n25.97\n4.432\n3.984\nTable 4: Results on GMO-SVS. Lower CER/WER/FFE and higher SIM/SingMOS/Sheet are better. Bold and underlined values\nindicate the best and second-best synthesized results, respectively; tied results receive the same formatting.\ngains, while target-space alignment provides additional com-\nplementary gains.\nAnchoring Depth.\nWe vary anchoring depth by attach-\ning the semantic prediction head after different Transformer\nblocks. Anchor@32 yields the lowest content errors, with a\nCER of 1.01 on ZH, a WER of 1.87 on EN, and a CER of\n11.87 on ZH-Hard. Anchor@24 increases these errors only\nslightly to 1.04, 1.93, and 12.01, while improving SIM from\n0.743, 0.687, and 0.697 to 0.747, 0.693, and 0.704, respec-\ntively. It also raises ZH-Hard UTMOS from 2.648 to 2.683.\nWe therefore use Anchor@24 for the system-level model be-\ncause it better balances content accuracy, speaker similarity,\nand perceptual quality. Overall, anchoring depth trades off\ncontent and quality metrics.\nAnchoring Strength.\nWe vary λsem to assess semantic\nsupervision strength. Increasing λsem from 0.1 to 0.5 wors-\nens English WER from 1.87 to 3.64 and reduces SIM from\n0.687 to 0.623. Setting λsem = 1.0 further degrades content,\nsimilarity, and UTMOS metrics. A smaller weight of 0.05\npreserves speaker similarity and perceptual quality but gives\nhigher content errors than 0.1. We therefore use λsem = 0.1,\nwhich minimizes content errors while maintaining compa-\nrable synthesis quality. These results suggest that seman-\ntic anchoring should regularize autoregressive state learning\nwithout dominating the continuous generation objective.\nSA-VAE Reconstruction under Semantic Alignment\nTa-\nble 3 compares SA-VAE reconstruction with representative\ndiscrete and continuous speech representations. At 25 Hz,\nSA-VAE achieves a PESQ of 2.99, a STOI of 0.96, and a UT-\nMOS score of 3.92. Its unaligned counterpart obtains nearly\nidentical scores of 3.00, 0.96, and 3.92, respectively, showing\nthat semantic alignment does not noticeably degrade acous-\ntic reconstruction. Despite different representation rates and\narchitectures, SA-VAE remains competitive with both con-\ntinuous and discrete tokenizers. These results support its role\nas a complementary target-space component that introduces\nsemantic alignment without imposing a discrete bottleneck\nor sacrificing reconstruction quality.\nCross-Task Transfer to Score-Conditioned SVS.\nTable 4\nevaluates semantic-token anchoring on score-conditioned\nSVS. Compared with the model without anchoring, Sem-\nBridge reduces Mandarin CER from 9.18 to 8.32 and English\nWER from 16.29 to 14.77, while speaker similarity remains\nessentially unchanged. SingMOS and Sheet scores also im-\nprove slightly in both languages, showing that improved lyric\nintelligibility does not compromise perceived singing qual-\nity or expressiveness. SemBridge further achieves the highest\nsynthesized SingMOS and Sheet scores among the compared\nsystems. Consistent gains across Mandarin and English sug-\ngest that the semantic constraint remains effective when lin-\nguistic prediction is coupled with score-derived pitch and\ntiming conditions. Its FFE results are not the best, indicating\nthat semantic-token anchoring mainly improves lyric model-\ning and perceptual quality rather than pitch accuracy. Over-\nall, the proposed supervision transfers beyond TTS without\nrequiring a task-specific semantic branch.\nConclusion\nIn this paper, we introduced SemBridge, a training-only\nsemantic-token anchoring framework for continuous-latent\nautoregressive speech generation. SemBridge directly super-\nvises autoregressive LM states with discrete semantic-token\nlabels, while a complementary Semantic-Aligned Acoustic\nVAE aligns continuous acoustic targets with embeddings\nfrom the same frozen semantic tokenizer. This shared se-\nmantic reference strengthens state-level linguistic modeling\nand target-space semantic correspondence while preserv-\ning continuous-only autoregressive inference. Experiments\non zero-shot TTS and score-conditioned SVS demonstrate\nthat SemBridge consistently improves content fidelity while\nmaintaining competitive speaker similarity and perceptual\nquality. Controlled ablations further confirm the complemen-\ntary roles of latent alignment and state anchoring and reveal a\ntrade-off effect of anchoring depth. Overall, semantic-token\nanchoring provides an effective training strategy for improv-\ning linguistic modeling in continuous-latent autoregressive\nspeech generation.\n\n=== PAGE 8 ===\nReferences\nAn, K.; Zhang, Z.; Gao, C.; Li, Y.; Peng, Z.; Wang, H.; Du,\nZ.; Zhao, H.; Gao, Z.; and Li, X. 2026. MELA-TTS: Joint\nTransformer-Diffusion Model with Representation Align-\nment for Speech Synthesis.\nIn Proceedings of the IEEE\nIn